{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "real or fake", "line": "Yeah, my brother get locked up in jail, I told him I’d bail him (Told him I’d—)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, my brother get locked up in jail, I told him I'd bail him (Told him I'd—)"], "meaning": "Yeat claims and implies that if one of his brothers were to be locked up in a jail cell, he would tell them they would be bailed out, courtesy of his substantial amount of money. This line follows the previous line in which Yeat describes the type of women that he encounters and hangs out with, possibly drug-misusers like Yeat implied by the description of them “sniffin' them lines of the white”.\n\nLines of white are interpreted as “Ellen”, being compared to the color of talk-show host Ellen DeGeneres’s skin. Sniffing white, therefore can be a reference to cocaine lines.\n\nThis line is similar to the opening chorus line of a leak by Yeat regarding the same topic, “Free em” in which he goes in-depth on the mental consequences drug/cocaine business and its misuse.\n\nhttps://pbs.twimg.com/media/FOfVHvLXIAEyovg.jpg:large", "annotation_id": "30124684", "url": "https://genius.com/30124684", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
