{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "përctivated", "line": "I’m on X and Percs everyday, I don’t know what to decide on", "vault_lines": ["I'm on X and Percs everyday, I don't know what to decide on"], "meaning": "Yeat is constantly on ecstasy and percocets and he doesn’t know which one to decide (as he has said in many other songs) or he is high and doesn’t and can’t use his common sense to decide on something.", "annotation_id": "30425302", "url": "https://genius.com/30425302", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "përctivated", "line": "Ate me up just like some Now and Laters", "vault_lines": ["And lil' shawty pulled up on me (Hey), ate me up just like some Now and Laters"], "meaning": "Now and Laters are a brand of candy chews in various fruity flavors. Yeat compares her “eating” him like eating some Now and Laters.", "annotation_id": "29681781", "url": "https://genius.com/29681781", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "përctivated", "line": "You were talking, I can’t be silent (Hey) I don’t talk, my phone on silent (Hey, yuh) No, don’t disturb me, I get violent (Hey, hey, whoo)", "vault_lines": ["You were talking, I can't be silent (Hey)"], "meaning": "When a member of Yeat’s opposition, or one of his his opps, starts talking crazy, Yeat does something about it.\n\nIn the first line, Yeat juxtaposes himself as the ever-looming greater threat to his opps; the nine-syllable line has a four-syllable portion for “you,” followed by five syllables of Yeat claiming to mimic your actions yet remaining intently vague on what he will actually do.\n\nThe next line continues the theme of juxtaposition when Yeat says he does not “talk,” which seemingly directly contradicts Yeat’s earlier statement when he says “[he] can’t be silent”. This, however, is not a contradiction but rather an implication that Yeat does not use words, but actions.\n\n\n\nThe third line references the Do Not Disturb feature common on most smartphones, which causes the phone to ignores all notifications while the feature is active. Yeat seems to use this feature often along with putting his ringtone on silent, and justifies doing so due to his tendencies to act out negatively as a response to others.", "annotation_id": "25407461", "url": "https://genius.com/25407461", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "përctivated", "line": "And my chain Minnesota (Huh, yeah, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["And my chain Minnesota (Huh, yeah, yeah)"], "meaning": "Minnesota, one of 50 states in the United States, is a place that has a reputation for being cold, often with freezing weather and precipitation in the winter months. International Falls, Minnesota, has the nickname “Icebox of the Nation”.\n\n\n\nThe above is Minnesota in 2019.\n\n“Cold,” “ice,” and other words referring to a state of low temperature are commonly used to refer to highly-valued jewelry or gems—in this case, Yeat’s chain is as cold as Minnesota, which once reached a low of -60 degrees Fahrenheit in 1996.\n\n\n\nAn “iced out” chain.", "annotation_id": "25407400", "url": "https://genius.com/25407400", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "përctivated", "line": "Hey, hey, need a respirator (Hey, hey) I been in my bag so much, I need a respirator (Hey, hey)", "vault_lines": ["Hey, hey, need a respirator (Hey, hey)"], "meaning": "In the first line, Yeat uses his vocal cadence and genius songwriting abilities to use his interjections of “hey” to sound out of breath, before saying he needs a respirator, which is a device for helping someone breathe.\n\n\n\nIt is highly advised never to place a plastic bag or any similar object over someone’s head due to the health risks associated with resultant asphyxiation. However, being “in [one’s] bag” is also a term for being heavily focused on one’s own personal profits and finances, with the term ‘bag’ stemming from the notion of a bag of money.\n\n\n\nIn the second line, Yeat claims to be in his bag so much, that it is causing him asphyxiation as heard in the first line.", "annotation_id": "25369906", "url": "https://genius.com/25369906", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
