{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "bigger thën everything", "line": "I could make the money come, I can make it go", "vault_lines": ["I could make the money come, I can make it go"], "meaning": "Yeat uses this lyric to emphasize his wealth, not only that but it could be a reference to a similar lyric off of Kanye West’s 2009 song “Paranoid”, where he says “I can make you come, I can make you go” It could be a subtle shoutout to one of Yeat’s childhood favorite artists, along the likes of T-Pain and Jay-Z.\n\nhttps://nshsdenebola.com/artist-spotlight-yeat/#:~:text=YEAT%20grew%20up%20listening%20to,big%20in%20the%20next%20year.", "annotation_id": "29644754", "url": "https://genius.com/29644754", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "bigger thën everything", "line": "I’m done layin’ back plottin’, I’m droppin’ everything, I’m droppin’ all these hits, yeah, yeah (I-I-I—)", "vault_lines": ["I'm done layin' back plottin', I'm droppin' everything, I'm droppin' all these hits, yeah, yeah (I-I-I—)"], "meaning": "Yeat has been working hard in the studio making projects and secretly building up his discography  of music. Now that he’s done with all the hard work he now want’s to reap the benefits of and now will slowly take over with dropping hit (music) after hit.", "annotation_id": "29577239", "url": "https://genius.com/29577239", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
