{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "bigger thën everything", "line": "They call me God, ’cause I’m as high as Him (’Cause I’m as high as Him)", "vault_lines": ["They call me God, 'cause I'm as high as Him ('Cause I'm as high as Him)"], "meaning": "Here, Yeat is referencing the use of drugs to achieve a heightened state of euphoria. He humorously compares his elevated state to being as high as God, playing on the double meaning of “high” both in terms of drug-induced euphoria and divine elevation.", "annotation_id": "29250928", "url": "https://genius.com/29250928", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "bigger thën everything", "line": "(I-I-I) We so mad, like devils in our head (I’m so high)", "vault_lines": ["We so mad, like devils in our head (I'm so high)"], "meaning": "Yeat often talks about his devils in his songs, and in some parts of the world, “mad” is another way of saying crazy. Basically Yeat is saying that he’s crazy with the devils in his head.", "annotation_id": "28616531", "url": "https://genius.com/28616531", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
