{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Wonky", "line": "Swiz need to pipe down", "vault_lines": ["(Ah) Patek goin' crazy"], "meaning": "SUBZEROSWIZ is an American producer most know for his work with Yeat", "annotation_id": "41439164", "url": "https://genius.com/41439164", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Wonky", "line": "This not Willy Wonka but this Wocky’ got me wonky (Wocky)", "vault_lines": ["This not Willy Wonka but this Wocky' got me wonky (Wocky)"], "meaning": "This is a wordplay chain. “Wocky” refers to Wockhardt, a brand often associated in rap with prescription cough syrup or lean. “Wonky” means dizzy, off-balance, or distorted, so he says the drink has him feeling strange. The Willy Wonka reference is mainly there to set up the “Wocky/wonky” soundplay.", "annotation_id": "40078914", "url": "https://genius.com/40078914", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Wonky", "line": "Got this soda, damn, I’m bouta pop, yeah, just like a cyst, yeah (Eughh)", "vault_lines": ["Got this soda, damn, I'm bouta pop, yeah, just like a cyst, yeah (Eughh)"], "meaning": "This is gross-out wordplay. “Soda” can point to lean or a mixed drink, and “pop” connects both to opening the drink and bursting a cyst. The line uses an unpleasant image to make the drug reference sound more vivid and exaggerated.", "annotation_id": "40078903", "url": "https://genius.com/40078903", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Wonky", "line": "Baby once you leave the coupe, with me, you don’t exist", "vault_lines": ["Baby once you leave the coupe with me, you don't exist"], "meaning": "The “coupe” represents his luxury car and the world around him. He tells the girl that once she leaves that space, she does not matter anymore. The line is cold and detached, showing how temporary and transactional the relationship is in the song’s lifestyle.", "annotation_id": "40078898", "url": "https://genius.com/40078898", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Wonky", "line": "Twizzy brought the blicky, he brought the lighter, he’s bouta’ spliff (Yeah, ba-ba-ba-ba)", "vault_lines": ["Twizzy brought the blicky, he brought the lighter, he's bouta' spliff (Yeah, ba-ba-ba-ba)"], "meaning": "This line mixes weapon and smoking imagery. “blicky” is likely slang for a firearm, while the lighter connects to smoking or “lighting up.” The wording creates a double meaning: his friend comes prepared for both partying and violence, which fits the song’s blend of reckless fun and street threat.", "annotation_id": "40078892", "url": "https://genius.com/40078892", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Wonky", "line": "Why you buggin’ out askin’ how much I make? (Yeah, what?) You know nothin’ ’bout the game or what the hell it takes (Shh), yeah", "vault_lines": ["Why you buggin' out askin' how much I make? (Yeah, what?)"], "meaning": "Yeat is annoyed at people asking about his money without understanding what it takes to earn it. “The game” refers to the lifestyle, industry, hustle, or street environment behind the success. He is basically saying outsiders see the money but do not understand the work, risk, or sacrifices behind it.", "annotation_id": "40078881", "url": "https://genius.com/40078881", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Wonky", "line": "Yeah, twizz on the run, we gotta be safe (Eyeah)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, twizz on the run, we gotta be safe (Eyeah)"], "meaning": "“Twizz” is slang for a close friend or twin-like associate. Saying he is “on the run” suggests movement, pressure, or trouble, so the rapper says they have to move carefully. The line adds street tension to the flexing, showing that the lifestyle is not just luxury but also danger and caution.", "annotation_id": "40078873", "url": "https://genius.com/40078873", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Wonky", "line": "(Ah) Patek goin’ crazy", "vault_lines": ["(Ah) Patek goin' crazy"], "meaning": "The opening “Patek” reference is a luxury flex. A Patek Philippe watch is treated as a symbol of serious money, so saying it is “going crazy” sets the tone immediately: the rapper is entering the chorus by showing off wealth, motion, and expensive taste.", "annotation_id": "40078862", "url": "https://genius.com/40078862", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Wonky", "line": "Button, bitch, don’t push my button (Button)", "vault_lines": ["Button, bitch, don't push my button (Button)"], "meaning": "Yeat references Benjamin Button, a fictional character who ages in reverse, born with the physical appearance of an elderly man and growing younger over time.\n\nThis could allude to the fact that regardless of his heavy drug use over the years and ever-increasing tolerance for substances, yeat does not feel like he’s aging", "annotation_id": "39395014", "url": "https://genius.com/39395014", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
