{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "U Should Know", "line": "Don’t talk ’bout me, yeah, I’m a fiend, I need Everything, money, cars, rings, I need", "vault_lines": ["Don't talk 'bout me, yeah, I'm a fiend, I need"], "meaning": "Yeat expresses a strong desire for material possessions and success.\n\nHe says “Don’t talk ‘bout me, yeah, I’m a fiend” to show that he has an intense desire or need for something, which could be success, recognition, or material wealth.\n\nThen he says “I need Everything, money, cars, rings, I need” listing a need for these things, further emphasizing his desire for a lifestyle of wealth and success.", "annotation_id": "31301761", "url": "https://genius.com/31301761", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "U Should Know", "line": "I just went rogue, yeah, don’t call up my phone Don’t text me ’bout shit, yeah, I don’t wanna know No, I don’t wanna know how far you could go, how low you could go I don’t wanna hear, if you live like me, then you lose control", "vault_lines": ["And I could, if I would"], "meaning": "“I just went rogue, yeah, don’t call up my phone”: The phrase “went rogue” often means to act independently or to deviate from expected behavior. Yeat might be expressing a decision to distance himself from others or a certain situation. The request not to call his phone emphasizes this desire for distance.\n\n“Don’t text me ‘bout shit, yeah, I don’t wanna know”: This line further emphasizes Yeat’s desire for separation. He doesn’t want to be contacted or informed about certain things, and he honestly doesn’t care what it is about either.\n\n“No, I don’t wanna know how far you could go, how low you could go”: Yeat is expressing that he doesn’t want to know the extents to which the person or situation can reach, whether it’s how far they can succeed (“how far you could go”) or how much they can fall off (“how low you could go”).\n\n“I don’t wanna hear, if you live like me, then you lose control”: This line suggests that living like Yeat, most likely referring to his lifestyle or choices, can lead to a loss of control. He doesn’t want to hear about others following his path and potentially facing the same challenges.", "annotation_id": "31301693", "url": "https://genius.com/31301693", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "U Should Know", "line": "You say you got this lifestyle but LYFESTYLE ain’t with it", "vault_lines": ["You say you got this lifestyle but LYFESTYLE ain't with it"], "meaning": "Yeat is addressing someone who claims to be living a certain “lifestyle” but then brings up his leaked title of an album called “Lyfestyle”", "annotation_id": "31301673", "url": "https://genius.com/31301673", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "U Should Know", "line": "I know you know, you think you know you know better I know you know, you think you know you know better (Woo) I know you know, you think you know you know better I think by now, you— you should know better", "vault_lines": ["I know you know, you think you know you know better"], "meaning": "A phonetically pleasing albeit highly esoteric way of saying “(I know) you (should) know better than to think you know”\n\nWhat it is that the addressed listener “thinks they know” is explored from various angles throughout this song.\n\n“You think you know Yeat”\n“You think you know you’re different”\n“You think you know what this Lyfestyle is”\netc.\n\nThis chorus is yet another example of Yeat’s unwavering emphasis on phonetics and de novo word-play with little to no regard for the average listener’s ability to understand what he is saying. A feature which Yeat himself takes pride in. E.g.:\n\n“They don’t understand what I’m saying, but that’s a win” – Yeat on ‘2day’", "annotation_id": "30984651", "url": "https://genius.com/30984651", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "U Should Know", "line": "In a pit with some snakes, and they bite", "vault_lines": ["In a pit with some snakes, and they bite"], "meaning": "The snakes represent Yeat’s fake friends who want to be his acquaintances due to his social status.", "annotation_id": "30977366", "url": "https://genius.com/30977366", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "U Should Know", "line": "Makin’ money, it feels so good Doing things that you never should", "vault_lines": ["Makin' money, it feels so good"], "meaning": "In the most narcissistic song on the album, we can see in this bar, “Makin' money, it feels so good”, rapper Yeat answers the most recent and frequently asked question of how he has been feeling with all this rapid growth in his career. We can easily identify how Yeat has felt good about this, and in the following line “Doing things that you never should” we can see him following the album’s theme, reflecting on self-care and believing in yourself by doing things that people deem impossible or useless. to do and even make a lot of money from it.", "annotation_id": "30931124", "url": "https://genius.com/30931124", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "U Should Know", "line": "Makin’ money, it feels so good Doing things that you never should", "vault_lines": ["Makin' money, it feels so good"], "meaning": "In the most narcissistic song on the album, we can see in this bar, “Makin' money, it feels so good”, rapper Yeat answers the most recent and frequently asked question of how he has been feeling with all this rapid growth in his career. We can easily identify how Yeat has felt good about this, and in the following line “Doing things that you never should” we can see him following the album’s theme, reflecting on self-care and believing in yourself by doing things that people deem impossible or useless to do and even make a lot of money from it.", "annotation_id": "30931102", "url": "https://genius.com/30931102", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
