{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tru to U", "line": "Yeah, that shit was sad, I could only afford to eat lunch meat", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, that shit was sad, I could only afford to eat lunch meat"], "meaning": "“You could say that I was down, but we up now”\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6dPMOotLMiQ", "annotation_id": "31960659", "url": "https://genius.com/31960659", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tru to U", "line": "Bitch not for me, she got that Pac-sun No that shit all night, for me, she got a rack or sumn’ Pockets the size of a building, all this drip on me no Pac-a-sun", "vault_lines": ["Bitch not for me, she got that Pac-sun"], "meaning": "Yeat is here saying that he cannot have any relationships with any woman who buys her clothes from Pac-sun, a store which is known to sell clothes.\n\nHe says that the bitch (female) only spent a rack ($1000) on her clothing at this store and this is simply not a lot of money for him. This is also a clever word play on the fact that she got her clothes from off the clothing rack at Pac-sun. Meaning she spend a rack on a rack of clothes. The average pricing for clothes at Pacsun is somewhere between $100-$120 meaning the woman likely obtained at least 10 pieces of clothing.\n\nHe then goes further to say that if you took all of his money and stacked it up his pockets would have to be the size of a building in order to fit all this money and the clothes he has on did not come from Pac-Sun because Yeat thinks that those clothes are simply too cheap for him.", "annotation_id": "30693325", "url": "https://genius.com/30693325", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tru to U", "line": "All this water that sit on me, collect like Hoover Dam (Yuh)", "vault_lines": ["All this water that sit on me, collect like Hoover Dam (Yuh)"], "meaning": "Hoover Dam is a the largest dam in the United States at full capacity. It’s located on the border between Nevada and Arizona. Yeat is saying he’s got as much diamonds in his chains and watches as water in Hoover Dam.\n\n.jpeg)", "annotation_id": "29362098", "url": "https://genius.com/29362098", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tru to U", "line": "Yeah, this a record label", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, this a record label"], "meaning": "TwizzyRich, formally known as TwizzyRich Entertainment, was a record label founded by Yeat. Before he was signed to Field Trip Recordings, Yeat used to release all of his music under his own label, most notably “Monëy so big” and “Sorry Bout That”.\n\nSome of TwizzyRich’s signees include American producer Trgc, who produced the aforementioned releases, and SeptembersRich, who featured in Yeat’s “Trëndy way,” becoming a mainstay in Yeat’s guest list.\n\n\n\nTwizzyRich signee SeptembersRich.", "annotation_id": "29071407", "url": "https://genius.com/29071407", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tru to U", "line": "Think I’m falling in love again (Yuh) With Benjamin Franklin, I’m sorry, ma’am", "vault_lines": ["Think I'm falling in love again (Yuh)"], "meaning": "Yeat puts a pause in between both of these lyrics to give it a suspense like feeling to the listener and/or apparent girl he is talking to.\n\nHe makes us think that the song is going to take a lovey-dovey approach to the rest of the song, then quickly lets us know that money is his only love while apologizing for any confusion.", "annotation_id": "28871594", "url": "https://genius.com/28871594", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tru to U", "line": "Yeah, and that bitch gon’ bounce on this dick like I got me hydraulics (Yuh)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and that bitch gon' bounce on this dick like I got me hydraulics (Yuh)"], "meaning": "Hydraulics in cars cause the the car to bounce up and down. Yeat uses this imagery to explain how the bitch is bouncing on his dick similar to that of a car with hydraulics.", "annotation_id": "28737038", "url": "https://genius.com/28737038", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tru to U", "line": "In the strip club, no Madagascar, I like the way that she move it, yeah (Like she move it)", "vault_lines": ["In the strip club, no Madagascar, I like the way that she move it, yeah (Like she move it)"], "meaning": "Reference to the iconic song in the Madagascar films: “I like to move it”.", "annotation_id": "28737020", "url": "https://genius.com/28737020", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tru to U", "line": "And my fingers blue, took a long time to get this way", "vault_lines": ["And my fingers blue, took a long time to get this way"], "meaning": "In this line, Yeat admits that it took a lot of effort to achieve the net worth he holds now, which is approximately $5 million.\n\n\n\nThe face of a $100 bill depicting Benjamin Franklin, one of the founding fathers of the United States. The blue security ribbon confirms that it’s not a counterfeit bill.\n\nBy claiming that his ‘fingers blue,’ he refers to having large amounts of $100 bills consistently on-hand, to the point where it might as well be his own fingers with a blue security ribbon.", "annotation_id": "28663652", "url": "https://genius.com/28663652", "state": "accepted", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
