{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Trië 2 Tell U outro", "line": "We humans, we gon’ cry", "vault_lines": ["I been in the sky, we humans, we gon' cry"], "meaning": "Yeat grapples with the inevitability of suffering in life", "annotation_id": "31773755", "url": "https://genius.com/31773755", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Trië 2 Tell U outro", "line": "Feelin’ thin boy, fettucine, swear I need me everything Yeah, overdose (Woo-ooh), yeah, overdose (Woo-ooh) Yeah, overdose (Woo-ooh), yeah, overdose", "vault_lines": ["Feelin' thin boy, fettucine, swear I need me everything"], "meaning": "Yeat expresses a sense of longing or intensity in his emotions, juxtaposed with a craving for fulfillment or completeness. When he says, “Feelin' deep ‘bout anything, but I need me everything,” he suggests that he experiences profound emotions or thoughts about various aspects of life, yet he feels a deep need for everything, implying a desire for completeness or wholeness. The repetition of “overdose” emphasizes the intensity of this longing or craving. It could suggest that Yeat feels overwhelmed by this desire, akin to the sensation of an overdose—experiencing an excess or overwhelming abundance of something, whether it be emotions, experiences, or material possessions. Overall, these lines convey a complex mix of deep emotion, longing for completeness, and a feeling of being overwhelmed by the intensity of these desires.", "annotation_id": "31021847", "url": "https://genius.com/31021847", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Trië 2 Tell U outro", "line": "It feel like an overdose is creepin’ up on me (Ah) My face is numb, I can’t feel nothin’, body numbin’ (Ah) I walked out the stu’ to get some air", "vault_lines": ["It feel like an overdose is creepin' up on me (Ah)"], "meaning": "the lines “It feel like an overdose is creeping up on me / My face is numb, I can’t feel nothin', body numbing / I walked out the stu' to get some air” convey Yeat’s visceral experience of drug intoxication and its physical effects. Yeat describes a sensation akin to an impending overdose, coupled with a profound numbness in his face and body, indicative of the potent influence of the drugs he’s consumed. The urgency of the situation prompts Yeat to leave the studio (“stu'”) in search of fresh air, reflecting a recognition of the need to remove himself from the environment and find relief from the overwhelming sensations. Overall, these lines capture a moment of distress and vulnerability for Yeat, illustrating the disorienting and potentially dangerous nature of drug use.", "annotation_id": "31021830", "url": "https://genius.com/31021830", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
