{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tried Once", "line": "Yeah you know it yea we the best Yeah you know Yeat in the studio with Blaze West or just Blaze for a day keep it real no new plays", "vault_lines": ["Yeah you know it yea we the best"], "meaning": "“Yeah you know it, yeah we the best”: This is a confident declaration, typical of rap, where the artist asserts their greatness. They’re letting everyone know they’re at the top of their game or that they’re among the best in what they do, whether it’s music, lifestyle, or influence.\n“Or just Blaze for a day”: This could be a nod to Blaze West , this line is likely saying that they are smoking\n“Keep it real no new plays”: This part suggests staying authentic and true to what works, rather than trying to be trendy or using gimmicks (“new plays”). The rapper is emphasizing that they are not changing their style to fit current trends but are instead sticking to what’s real and true to themselves.", "annotation_id": "35098848", "url": "https://genius.com/35098848", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tried Once", "line": "Run a blitz in his face Get a sack then get a raise", "vault_lines": ["Run a blitz in his face"], "meaning": "The line “Run a blitz in his face, get a sack then get a raise” combines football terminology with a metaphor about success and achieving goals.\n\n\n“Run a blitz in his face”: In football, a “blitz” is a defensive strategy where players (usually linebackers or safeties) rush toward the quarterback to pressure them, often with the intent of causing a sack. “Blitz in his face” suggests aggressively confronting or pressuring someone, either literally or metaphorically.\n“Get a sack”: In football, a “sack” refers to tackling the quarterback behind the line of scrimmage, preventing them from making a successful play. In the context of the rap line, “getting a sack” could symbolize overcoming a challenge, handling a situation, or achieving a win, particularly when facing pressure.\n“Then get a raise”: This part connects the football metaphor to real-life success. After executing the blitz (taking action and overcoming a challenge) and getting a sack (securing a win), the rapper claims they “get a raise,” implying that their success will be rewarded, whether it’s in terms of money, recognition, or promotion.", "annotation_id": "35098845", "url": "https://genius.com/35098845", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tried Once", "line": "Put me and Yeat in the same room and we keep getting higher", "vault_lines": ["Put me and Yeat in the same room and we keep getting higher"], "meaning": "The line “Put me and Yeat in the same room and we keep getting higher” is a clever wordplay that uses both a physical and metaphorical meaning.\n\n\nPhysical Meaning – Getting Higher: The phrase “getting higher” could refer to being in an elevated space, like being in a room above ground level (such as a high-rise room). In this case, it’s a fun way of saying that just by being in the same room, they’re on a different level literally and figuratively.\nMetaphorical Meaning – Success and Energy: “Getting higher” is also a common metaphor in hip-hop, where it can refer to rising in success, reaching new levels, or achieving greater heights in terms of fame, wealth, and influence. It could also allude to “getting higher” as in getting more energized or motivated, as if the collaboration or energy between the rapper and Yeat leads to an increase in their creative power or success.", "annotation_id": "35098824", "url": "https://genius.com/35098824", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tried Once", "line": "We are like the Lakers we Kobe and Shaq (Kobe and Shaq)", "vault_lines": ["We are like the Lakers we Kobe and Shaq (Kobe and Shaq)"], "meaning": "The line “We are like the Lakers, we Kobe and Shaq” is a reference to the iconic partnership between Kobe Bryant and Shaquille O'Neal, two of the greatest basketball players in history, who played together for the Los Angeles Lakers in the late ‘90s and early 2000s. They won three consecutive NBA championships together (2000, 2001, 2002).\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n\nLakers Reference: The Lakers are one of the most successful and famous basketball teams in NBA history. Associating themselves with the Lakers signals that the rapper is part of a winning team, someone who is part of something big, successful, and legendary.\nKobe and Shaq: Kobe Bryant and Shaquille O'Neal were a dominant duo. Though they had different playing styles and personalities, together they formed an unstoppable force on the court. “Kobe and Shaq” symbolize an unbeatable partnership. In the context of the rap bar, it’s a metaphor for a strong, powerful, and successful collaboration or bond between the rapper and their partner (or team).", "annotation_id": "35098818", "url": "https://genius.com/35098818", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
