{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "The Wolf", "line": "Just like one of them Chargers you got gas, all of that ammunition inside of it How many Urus you think I got in my side garages, bitch?", "vault_lines": ["Just like one of them Chargers you got gas, all of that ammunition inside of it"], "meaning": "Yeat uses clever but obfuscatory (unclear, hard to understand) wordplay to get his point across.\n\nThe line starts with him stating “Just like one of them Chargers, you got gas.” This references the Dodge Charger, a gas-operated car that costs around $47,000 to $78,000 depending on the model.\n\nIt then ends with “all of that ammunition inside it,” which is a broad metaphor, but probably means that the other person’s car is “shot,” or run down, and that now it’s simply just a display piece, not for actual use. This leads into his next line, where he notes that he has plenty of Lamborghini Uruses in his side garage, so he doesn’t need to keep or use one as an antique.", "annotation_id": "39516345", "url": "https://genius.com/39516345", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "The Wolf", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Yeat-the-wolf-lyrics", "genius_title": "The Wolf", "genius_id": 12930876, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "The Last Supper", "line": "I seen 2093 enough for all of us I seen Hell and came back, enough for all of us I had to wipe my hands of all this blood, ’cause I’m a sinner", "vault_lines": ["I seen 2093 enough for all of us"], "meaning": "Yeat is probably saying that he was ahead of time and knew he was going to be rich when he released 2093 or after he became more famous (“I seen 2093 enough for all of us”). And he is most certainly saying that he knew that because he was dealing with Lyfë and drugs problems daily (“I seen Hell and came back”) and at the same time he was getting rid of it “I had to wipe my hands of all this blood, ‘cause I’m a sinner”.", "annotation_id": "40564214", "url": "https://genius.com/40564214", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "The Last Supper", "line": "I’m a Lyfestyle man, I’m crazy, I’m a C.E.O, you can pay me", "vault_lines": ["I'm a Lyfestyle man, I'm crazy, I'm a C.E.O, you can pay me"], "meaning": "This is a reference to two of Yeat’s later albums.\n\nIn Lyfestyle, the premise of the album is crazy- bouncing 808s and wild synths.\n\nIn 2093, his alter-ego is the Psycho C.E.O., who has billions of dollars, hence “you can pay me.”", "annotation_id": "36879953", "url": "https://genius.com/36879953", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "The Last Supper", "line": "All these people’s hands out for presents, it’s not December (Aah)", "vault_lines": ["All these people's hands out for presents, it's not December (Aah)"], "meaning": "This double entendre encapsulates people’s obsession with only being around yeat when he is benefiting them, or “handing out” something. Their “hands out” like it’s December is a reference to Christmas, which is on December 25th, and usually when people’s hands are out for gifts and such.", "annotation_id": "36879937", "url": "https://genius.com/36879937", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "The Last Supper", "line": "I’m an asshole and I’m sorry for it, but I’m not", "vault_lines": ["I'm an asshole and I'm sorry for it, but I'm not"], "meaning": "#washedinselfsin", "annotation_id": "34846824", "url": "https://genius.com/34846824", "state": "pending", "votes": -3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "The Last Supper", "line": "I had to wipe my hands of all this blood, ’cause I’m a sinner", "vault_lines": ["I had to wipe my hands of all this blood, 'cause I'm a sinner"], "meaning": "Yeat is talking about having to wash away the sins he has committed. However, in a Christian context, only Jesus has the power to do that.", "annotation_id": "34787569", "url": "https://genius.com/34787569", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
