{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tha Worstie", "line": "’cause y’all some birdies", "vault_lines": ["We really some duck hunters man, 'cause y'all some birdies"], "meaning": "A “bird” is slang for someone who is copying your sound, similar to a parrot. Yeat implies that he uses violent measures to eliminate anyone immitating him to keep his style fresh.", "annotation_id": "29510206", "url": "https://genius.com/29510206", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tha Worstie", "line": "She huffin’ and puffin’ and blow, that bih’ like the big bad wolf", "vault_lines": ["She huffin' and puffin' and blow, that bih' like the big bad wolf"], "meaning": "The Big Bad Wolf is a popular childrens story which describes the importance of building up strong foundations in case hardship comes your way. In the story, a wolf woke up with a hankering for some pork, so he tries to eat three pigs. The pigs make three houses to stop the wolf from eating them. The first one is made out of straw, and the wolf is easily able to destroy the house. The second one is made out of sticks, and is also destroyed with relative ease. The third house however, is made out of bricks, and the wolf is unable to blow it down. Yeat uses a play on words by saying a girl is “blowing” on him like the big bad wolf. It also implies that he is as hard as bricks, since this girl is supposedly unable to knock him down by blowing on him, although there isn’t a followup line in the song.", "annotation_id": "29510180", "url": "https://genius.com/29510180", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tha Worstie", "line": "I seen a T, turn that bitch upside down", "vault_lines": ["I seen a T, turn that bitch upside down"], "meaning": "An upside T represents an inverted cross- often associated with Satanism and the devil in Christianity.\n\n\n\nYeat continues the previous line by saying he uses inverted crosses to protect himself. He has claimed that he “sold his soul” to the devil on multiple occasions, which is a moderately popular phenomenon in rap, commonly associated with Playboi Carti, who used an inverted cross on the set of his “Whole Lotta Red” or “King Vamp” tour.", "annotation_id": "25521309", "url": "https://genius.com/25521309", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
