{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "I don’t wish you well, yeah, I don’t give a fuck", "vault_lines": ["I don't wish you well, yeah, I don't give a fuck"], "meaning": "This is drugged Yeat fighting sober Yeat. He doesn’t care about himself.", "annotation_id": "33047210", "url": "https://genius.com/33047210", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "It’s easier to scream, I’ll send you straight to hell", "vault_lines": ["It's easier to scream, I'll send you straight to hell"], "meaning": "“It’s easier to scream” – This could imply that expressing emotions (like frustration, anger, or pain) through loud or extreme reactions (screaming) is simpler than dealing with them in a more calm or controlled way. It suggests a sense of urgency, chaos, or perhaps feeling overwhelmed.\n\n“I’ll send you straight to hell” – This is a threat or a declaration of power. “Hell” in this context likely represents destruction, punishment, or suffering. The speaker is asserting dominance, suggesting that they have the ability to severely harm or punish someone, either metaphorically or literally.", "annotation_id": "33047207", "url": "https://genius.com/33047207", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "Tell me what else, tell me your tale", "vault_lines": ["Tell me what else, tell me your tale"], "meaning": "“Tell me what else, tell me your tale” – Here, Yeat could be inviting the listener (or someone else) to share their own story or journey. It’s a moment where he’s reflecting on his own experience and perhaps opening up to hear others' perspectives.", "annotation_id": "33047202", "url": "https://genius.com/33047202", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "Picture to a frame, yeah, I ain’t gon’ change", "vault_lines": ["Picture to a frame, yeah, I ain't gon' change"], "meaning": "“Picture to a frame, yeah, I ain’t gon' change” – The metaphor of a “picture to a frame” might symbolize how he fits perfectly in his current role, almost like a picture in a frame fits seamlessly. It also implies that he’s found his place and purpose, and that he doesn’t intend to change who he is despite his success.", "annotation_id": "33047199", "url": "https://genius.com/33047199", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "Flying ’round the world (whoa-oh-ah), married to the game", "vault_lines": ["Flying 'round the world (whoa-oh-ah), married to the game"], "meaning": "“Flying ‘round the world (whoa-oh-ah), married to the game” – This line indicates Yeat’s lifestyle of constant travel, possibly due to his career in music. “Married to the game” suggests his deep commitment to the music industry or hustle, implying that he sees his career as a lifelong partnership, much like a marriage.", "annotation_id": "33047195", "url": "https://genius.com/33047195", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "Clinging on the sheets Falling down for weeks History repeats", "vault_lines": ["Clinging on the sheets"], "meaning": "Yeat references his lyric in ‘'If We Being Real’‘ (i keep on chasing the highs, i know i could never get back so im always on low) Clinging on the sheets = Chasing the highs, Falling down for weeks = knowing he could never get back, History repeats = … history, repeats.", "annotation_id": "33047143", "url": "https://genius.com/33047143", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "I don’t need to see, yeah, I don’t need to hear I don’t wanna talk to you, yeah,", "vault_lines": ["I don't need to see, yeah, I don't need to hear"], "meaning": "This is likely a reference to the phrase, “see no evil, hear no evil, speak no evil”.", "annotation_id": "32904058", "url": "https://genius.com/32904058", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "Look me in the eyes, don’t tell me what you see", "vault_lines": ["Look me in the eyes, don't tell me what you see"], "meaning": "Yeat could be expressing a desire for honesty despite the inner turmoil he’s experiencing. He may be challenging others to confront the reality of his situation rather than offering superficial reassurances.", "annotation_id": "32721749", "url": "https://genius.com/32721749", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "I love Hell, Hell on earth", "vault_lines": ["I love Hell, Hell on earth"], "meaning": "This could indicate a contradictory or self-destructive sentiment towards drug use. On one hand, “loving Hell” might suggest a certain attraction to the intense experiences associated with drug use, such as altered states of consciousness or temporary relief from emotional pain. However, acknowledging it as “Hell on earth” indicates an awareness of the negative consequences and suffering that accompany substance abuse.", "annotation_id": "32672080", "url": "https://genius.com/32672080", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "Write my sins off, yeah, know the blood better", "vault_lines": ["Write my sins off, yeah, know the blood better"], "meaning": "This line is a reference to the Christian belief that Jesus’s blood can cleanse sin. Yeat explains that his connection to God absolves him from those that might consider him a sinner. Biblical references like this come up regularly during 2093’s runtime.", "annotation_id": "32666559", "url": "https://genius.com/32666559", "state": "accepted", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "I been out for weeks, it get easier for me", "vault_lines": ["I been out for weeks, it get easier for me"], "meaning": "Yeat is expressing that he has grown accustomed to this state of constant turmoil in his life brought on by excessive substance abuse.", "annotation_id": "32657839", "url": "https://genius.com/32657839", "state": "accepted", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "But don’t tell me, “Go help yourself,” you can go and die", "vault_lines": ["But don't tell me, \"Go help yourself,\" you can go and die"], "meaning": "People struggling with addiction often feel alone and isolated in their struggle. Yeat is expressing anger towards a statement he may have once been told by someone.", "annotation_id": "31608250", "url": "https://genius.com/31608250", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "Eat an organ, you live forever Yeah, I was born to live forever", "vault_lines": ["Eat an organ, you live forever"], "meaning": "Yeat is throwing shade at artists who had to sell their souls to forever be remembered. Eating an organ to live forever is yet another biblical metaphor for selling your soul to the devil. Yeat, however, was born naturally with the talent to forever be remember through his music.", "annotation_id": "30928603", "url": "https://genius.com/30928603", "state": "accepted", "votes": 47, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tell më", "line": "Turn the wheel to the left, right, not better", "vault_lines": ["Turn the wheel to the left, right, not better"], "meaning": "Left-hand path and right-hand path are two opposing approaches to magic.\n\nIn some definitions, the left-hand path is equated with malicious black magic, while the right-hand path is equated with benevolent white magic.\n\nThe figure of Baphomet, as depicted by Éliphas Lévi in Dogme et Rituel de la Haute Magie (1856), has been adopted as a symbol of left-hand path belief systems. This is a continuation of the biblical themes and references found throughout the project.", "annotation_id": "30900417", "url": "https://genius.com/30900417", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
