{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "“Can you help my money, make it taller?”", "vault_lines": ["\"Can you help my money, make it taller?\" (Oh, yeah, oh, yeah, oh, yeah)"], "meaning": "To make money “taller” is a new term popularised by Lil Uzi Vert and Yeat amongst others, meaning that if they were to stack their money on top of eachother, making money “taller” would simply mean heightening the stack, thereby increasing their income.", "annotation_id": "40275901", "url": "https://genius.com/40275901", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "Flagrant", "vault_lines": ["When I'm fuckin' this bitch, she gon' foul language, I'ma call this bitch flagrant"], "meaning": "Flagrant is an adjective that describes something shockingly noticeable or blatantly offensive.", "annotation_id": "40275896", "url": "https://genius.com/40275896", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "She give head like a scholar", "vault_lines": ["She give head like a scholar"], "meaning": "“Head” refers to oral sex.\n“Like a scholar” refers to performing a task with proficiency, concentration, or knowledge.\n\nThus, the line essentially conveys that she is extremely skilled at it, as if she has “studied” it or is knowledgeable about it.", "annotation_id": "39186659", "url": "https://genius.com/39186659", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "They say, “Tonka” (Huh? Huh?)", "vault_lines": ["They say, \"Tonka\" (Huh, huh)"], "meaning": "While tonka’s original definition is a big-body luxury truck/SUV, Yeat has now nicknamed himself as Tonka/King Tonka. Obviously he is a human and not a car, so this is probably a way to say he is really rich by having all the tonka’s.\n\nHe has used this nickname before in various tracks:\n\nKing tonka\n\nIt king Tonka (Err, err), it king Tonka (Err, err), it king T—\n\nLet King Tonka Talk\n\nHold on, yeah, let King Tonka talk\n\nGrowing Pains\n\nKing Tonka, King Tonka (What?) Tell me like King Tonka, King Tonka", "annotation_id": "39169640", "url": "https://genius.com/39169640", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "They say, “Tonka” (Huh, huh)", "vault_lines": ["They say, \"Tonka\" (Huh, huh)"], "meaning": "While tonka’s original definition is a big-body luxury truck/SUV, Yeat has now nicknamed himself as Tonka/King Tonka. Obviously he is a human and not a car, so this is probably a way to say he is really rich by having all the tonka’s.\n\nHe has used this nickname before in various tracks:\n\nKing tonka\n\nIt king Tonka (Err, err), it king Tonka (Err, err), it king T—\n\nLet King Tonka Talk\n\nHold on, yeah, let King Tonka talk\n\nGrowing Pains\n\nKing Tonka, King Tonka (What?) Tell me like King Tonka, King Tonka", "annotation_id": "39164559", "url": "https://genius.com/39164559", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "Bidness (Uh-huh)", "vault_lines": ["Throw a swing and, yeah, I'll revolve 'em"], "meaning": "“bidness” is the African American Vernacular English (AAVE) spelling/pronunciation of “business\".", "annotation_id": "39164531", "url": "https://genius.com/39164531", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "Range Rover (What?)", "vault_lines": ["Throw a swing and, yeah, I'll revolve 'em"], "meaning": "Yeat has mentioned the Land Rover Range Rover multiple times through out his discography. It’s a way to brag about his money.\n\nDiëd B4\n\nPullin' up in Range Rover trucks, this shit so greasy\n\nFeelinz\n\npeeled off in Range Rover Cutlass, diamonds flushing\n\nIf I died\n\nRange Rover but I’m perfectly steerin'\n\nInsane\n\nI was in the Range Rover, it took two planes\n\nKinda krazy\n\nI done crashed the Range Rover (Swerve, err)\n\nPerc Ten\n\nRange Rover, my back, that’s why I am not lookin'\n\nPower Trip\n\nyou a Range Rover", "annotation_id": "39164478", "url": "https://genius.com/39164478", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "High with the birds", "vault_lines": ["High with the birds"], "meaning": "Yeat has used this line before in Type monëy:\n\nI’m sippin' on lean, lean, no green, I’m high as a bird, bitch (Phew)\n\nYeat is so high (under the influence) that he is right next to birds (high in the sky).", "annotation_id": "39164383", "url": "https://genius.com/39164383", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "I’m, I’m Cuttin’ ties, cu— cuttin’, cuttin’ ties To make my money taller than ya, uh, I’m cuttin’ ties (Cuttin’ ties, cuttin’, cuttin’) To make my money taller than ya, uh, I’m cuttin’ ties (Ties, cuttin’ ties) To make my money taller than ya, uh (Cuttin’, cuttin’ ties, cuttin’ ties, cuttin’ ties, cuttin’)", "vault_lines": ["Cuttin' ties, cu— cuttin', cuttin' ties"], "meaning": "Yeat directly links financial growth with personal sacrifice. “Cutting ties” implies abandoning relationships or connections in order to prioritize success, highlighting one of the album’s core ideas: wealth often comes at the cost of human connection.", "annotation_id": "39149276", "url": "https://genius.com/39149276", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "She say, “Tonka, how you left the Earth?”", "vault_lines": ["She say, \"Tonka, how you left the Earth?\""], "meaning": "“Left the Earth” suggests being so elevated—through money, fame, or drugs—that he’s no longer grounded in reality. It reinforces the album’s recurring imagery of detachment and living above normal human experience.", "annotation_id": "39149274", "url": "https://genius.com/39149274", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "You been a seagull with the birds", "vault_lines": ["You been a seagull with the birds"], "meaning": "The “bird” imagery continues from the 6th song: “Liv Likë Dis,” but here it takes on a more dismissive tone. By calling someone a “seagull,” Yeat reduces them to something noisy and insignificant, suggesting they’re part of the same lifestyle but without real value or status.", "annotation_id": "39149265", "url": "https://genius.com/39149265", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "I’m the problem solver (Oh, oh)", "vault_lines": ["I'm the problem solver (Oh, oh)"], "meaning": "Yeat presents himself as someone who handles everything: money, conflicts, and relationships. However, within the context of ADL, this confidence contrasts with his growing instability, raising the question of whether he’s truly solving problems or creating new ones.", "annotation_id": "39149253", "url": "https://genius.com/39149253", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "“Can you help my money, make it taller?” (Oh, yeah, oh, yeah, oh, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["\"Can you help my money, make it taller?\" (Oh, yeah, oh, yeah, oh, yeah)"], "meaning": "“Taller” is used as a metaphor for growth and accumulation of wealth. Instead of simply wanting more money, Yeat frames it as something that constantly needs to expand, suggesting that success is never enough and always requires further escalation.", "annotation_id": "39149249", "url": "https://genius.com/39149249", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "And I cover my face with the money, I’m back on the turb’ (What? What?)", "vault_lines": ["And I cover my face with the money, I'm back on the turb' (What? What?)"], "meaning": "Yeat always wear a turban on his old eras. And he says, what he cover her face and money by turban.", "annotation_id": "39147465", "url": "https://genius.com/39147465", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "And I cover my face with the money, I’m back on the turb’ (What? What?)", "vault_lines": ["And I cover my face with the money, I'm back on the turb' (What? What?)"], "meaning": "Yeat always wear a turban on his old eras. And he says, what he cover her face and money by turban.", "annotation_id": "39147429", "url": "https://genius.com/39147429", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "She give head like a scholar (Huh? Huh?)", "vault_lines": ["She give head like a scholar"], "meaning": "A scholar is a highly educated person, typically specialized in a specific field of study. In this line, Yeat could be conveying one of two ideas:\n\n1) The girl giving him head has studied the art of oral sex; hence, she is a master of the craft.\n\nor\n\n2) A “scholar” is sharp and intelligent. Yeat could be flipping this, comparing a scholar’s “good head” (intellect) to the girl giving him good head (skill).\n\nIt’s worth noting that Yeat may be referencing the line, “Your bitch gives me brain like a scholar,” from his track “Sidëwayz” on the mixtape 4L. He may also be alluding to the bar, “Good college, she got good brain, she got good knowledge,” from “Narcoticz” on 2 Alivë (Geëk Pack).", "annotation_id": "39144103", "url": "https://genius.com/39144103", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Tallër", "line": "Two guns, not no revolver (Brr, brr, baow, baow) Throw a swing and, yeah, I’ll revolve ’em", "vault_lines": ["Throw a swing and, yeah, I'll revolve 'em"], "meaning": "Possibly a double entendre where Yeat says: “Two guns not no ‘volver\nIn the next bar: \"Throw a swing yeah, I’ll revolve em”\n\nThe first bar is relating to a revolver and in the second one, “throwing a swing” in golf terms, where the golfer releases their hands and clubhead early in the downswing, mimicking the natural motion of throwing a ball underhand to maximize speed-also in a revolving motion\n\nYeat is also seen having interest in golf and playing golf from a November 2025 video", "annotation_id": "39143491", "url": "https://genius.com/39143491", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
