{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "All your diamonds fake, bitch, they ain’t glarin’ (Ah)", "vault_lines": ["All your diamonds fake, bitch, they ain't glarin' (Ah)"], "meaning": "Here, he mocks others for flaunting fake jewelry, contrasting their inauthenticity with his own wealth and genuine success.", "annotation_id": "33961440", "url": "https://genius.com/33961440", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "I was impaired, I don’t like sunglasses, why you starin’?", "vault_lines": ["I was impaired, I don't like sunglasses, why you starin'?"], "meaning": "For Yeat, saying “I don’t like sunglasses” reflects his raw and unfiltered approach to life, suggesting he doesn’t feel the need to hide behind an image or shield himself, even when “impaired,” reinforcing his confidence in being unapologetically himself. While calling out those who stare at him, suggesting their judgment is unwelcome.", "annotation_id": "33961431", "url": "https://genius.com/33961431", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "Shut up, bitch, don’t tell me (Ah)", "vault_lines": ["Shut up, bitch, don't tell me (Ah)"], "meaning": "Yeat directly rejects the negativity or advice, signaling his unwillingness to entertain opinions that don’t align with his vision or self-image.", "annotation_id": "33961383", "url": "https://genius.com/33961383", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "They tell me when you talk ’bout me, they tell me (Ha)", "vault_lines": ["They tell me when you talk 'bout me, they tell me (Ha)"], "meaning": "This line suggests Yeat is aware of gossip or criticism directed at him, but the dismissive “Ha” shows he finds it amusing rather than concerning.", "annotation_id": "33961381", "url": "https://genius.com/33961381", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "They tell me you too much for me, they tell me (Ooh-ah, shh)", "vault_lines": ["They tell me you too much for me, they tell me (Ooh-ah, shh)"], "meaning": "Here, he emphasizes that it’s not just one person but multiple people who feel intimidated or overpowered by him, highlighting his distinctiveness and the impact he has on others. He then laughs it off and tells them to be quiet.", "annotation_id": "33961379", "url": "https://genius.com/33961379", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "(She tell me, ”You too much for me”, she tells me, ha-ha)", "vault_lines": ["They tell me you too much for me, they tell me (Ooh-ah, shh)"], "meaning": "For Yeat, this line reflects how his larger-than-life persona, wealth, or lifestyle can overwhelm others, but he brushes it off with a laugh, showing indifference to their judgment.", "annotation_id": "33961377", "url": "https://genius.com/33961377", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "Yeah, we got the motherfuckin’ money, we pull up outside, we leave ’em embarrassed (Ooh)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, we got the motherfuckin' money, we pull up outside, we leave 'em embarrassed (Ooh)"], "meaning": "Portraying how his flashy arrival not only displays his success but also humiliates those who can’t measure up", "annotation_id": "33961369", "url": "https://genius.com/33961369", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "Call on me, out the Maybach pull up on me (Ooh)", "vault_lines": ["Call on me, out the Maybach pull up on me (Ooh)"], "meaning": "This line highlights Yeat’s wealth and high status, using the Maybach—a symbol of luxury and power—as a personal emblem of his elevated position and readiness to provide or show up when needed.", "annotation_id": "33961366", "url": "https://genius.com/33961366", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "Huh, no we don’t fuck with them broke boys, baby, we don’t know them (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Huh, no we don't fuck with them broke boys, baby, we don't know them (Huh)"], "meaning": "For Yeat, this line emphasizes his separation from those who lack success or ambition.", "annotation_id": "33961362", "url": "https://genius.com/33961362", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
