{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "Salad with these diamonds", "vault_lines": ["Salad with these diamonds"], "meaning": "Yeat is using “salad” as a metaphor for the abundance and variety of his diamonds, similar to how a salad contains a mix of different ingredients.", "annotation_id": "33448903", "url": "https://genius.com/33448903", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "(She tell me (More), ”You too much for me”, she tells me, ha-ha) They tell me you too much for me, they tell me (Ooh-ah, shh) They tell me when you talk ’bout me, they tell me (Ha) Shut up, bitch, don’t tell me (Ah)", "vault_lines": ["They tell me you too much for me, they tell me (Ooh-ah, shh)"], "meaning": "Yeat seems to be addressing the mixed messages he receives from people. The repetition of being told he’s “too much” reflects how others view his lyfestyle as overwhelming. The line about people talking about him suggests he’s aware of the gossip and opinions circulating about him. His response telling them to “shut up” indicates he’s tired of the negativity and doesn’t want to hear it anymore.", "annotation_id": "33448866", "url": "https://genius.com/33448866", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "Yeah, when we at the club, whole streets outside", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, when we at the club, whole streets outside"], "meaning": "Yeat is saying he has influence and popularity. When he says “whole streets outside,” it shows that his presence at the club attracts a massive crowd, implying that people are eager to see him and be part of the scene.", "annotation_id": "33448817", "url": "https://genius.com/33448817", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "Yeah, slide on me and the family members, R.I.P. him", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, slide on me and the family members, R.I.P. him"], "meaning": "The “slide on me and the family members, R.I.P. him” could be an allusion to the metaphorical and literal meaning of loss or betrayal. The sentence could mean that Yeat refers to the close ties-like family members who are his support but at the same time endure risks with him. “Slide on me” might then insinuate that the struggles or conflicts coming his way affect not just him but also his “family members.”\n\nThe phrase “R.I.P. him” could symbolize the consequences of such a lifestyle in which conflict is part of reality-one that could claim a price for loyalty and survival. In this case, Yeat might be paying his respects to those he’s lost along the way. This makes the line both a warning and homage to the cost of his loyalty and resilience.\n\nThat’s a big mix of loyalty, risk, and respect combined, as Yeat just shows that his closest circle shares his struggles along with the losses.", "annotation_id": "33437743", "url": "https://genius.com/33437743", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "Yeah, slide on me and the family members, R.I.P. him", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, slide on me and the family members, R.I.P. him"], "meaning": "The “slide on me and the family members, R.I.P. him” could be an allusion to the metaphorical and literal meaning of loss or betrayal. The sentence could mean that Yeat refers to the close ties-like family members who are his support but at the same time endure risks with him. “Slide on me” might then insinuate that the struggles or conflicts coming his way affect not just him but also his “family members.”\n\nThe phrase “R.I.P. him” could symbolize the consequences of such a lifestyle in which conflict is part of reality-one that could claim a price for loyalty and survival. In this case, Yeat might be paying his respects to those he’s lost along the way. This makes the line both a warning and homage to the cost of his loyalty and resilience.\n\nThat’s a big mix of loyalty, risk, and respect combined, as Yeat just shows that his closest circle shares his struggles along with the losses.", "annotation_id": "33437740", "url": "https://genius.com/33437740", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "I was impaired, I don’t like sunglasses, why you starin’?", "vault_lines": ["I was impaired, I don't like sunglasses, why you starin'?"], "meaning": "Yeat is talking about feeling impaired due to substances. He mentions liking sunglasses, which can be a way to hide his eyes. When he asks why people are staring, it suggests he’s aware of the attention he’s getting and maybe feeling judged.", "annotation_id": "33417520", "url": "https://genius.com/33417520", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "Follow me, even if you couldn’t see shit (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Follow me, even if you couldn't see shit (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Yeat is saying that real friends or followers will trust him and stick with him no matter what, even if they can’t see what’s ahead. It’s about having faith in him without needing visible proof.", "annotation_id": "33417506", "url": "https://genius.com/33417506", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "I don’t know how I’m alive, I had two loans on it, loans on it", "vault_lines": ["(Ooh-ah) I don't know how I'm alive, I had two loans on it, loans on it"], "meaning": "Having debt to the wrong type of person can be fatal. In some cases if someone wants to maintain their reputation as “Not to be F***ed With”, they may be willing to kill you as an example to other people who owe them larger sums of money.\n\nNoah was likely in this situation and is reflecting on how his rise to stardom saved his life by allowing his loans to be forgiven one way or another.", "annotation_id": "33389841", "url": "https://genius.com/33389841", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "All your fuckin streams, ain’t playin, yeah, you fake it", "vault_lines": ["All your fuckin streams, ain't playin, yeah, you fake it"], "meaning": "While the lyric here is “you uh fake it” Yeat purposefully slurs his pronunciation so listeners can hear him say the homophobic slur “you a f*****”. This is not a coincidence and is found to be humorous by Yeat fans as it shows his consistent unseriousness but professionalism. He also says throughout the album that he cannot be cancelled, simply due to lack of caring.", "annotation_id": "33366184", "url": "https://genius.com/33366184", "state": "pending", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "THEY TELL MË", "line": "Yeah, I put the bills on everything, like I put them ions on it", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I put the bills on everything, like I put them ions on it"], "meaning": "The line sounds more like “put dem Yos on it”. When you have enough money to throw around, even strangers can quickly become your employees. By putting bills (100’s) in somebody’s hand you don’t really need to know their name. You can just call them “Yo”. Yos are typically younger, money hungry individuals willing to do hard labor.\n\nThe next verse perpetuates the sentiment of this line. As Yeat claims, “no one f wit dem fboys baby, don’t know them.” he is reinforcing that inferior men are doomed to rename nameless.\n\nBecause the overall theme of the song lies in the use of the pronouns “they” & “them” to describe individuals we’ll never know about, Noah’s use of the term “Yos” aligns with the motif. The F-boys mentioned in the next line can be carelessly disposed of by opportunists of any stature and no one would really care.", "annotation_id": "33354056", "url": "https://genius.com/33354056", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
