{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Still countin", "line": "Fuck a crib, I’m ’bout to hop on a jet and just live in the sky (’Ky)", "vault_lines": ["Fuck a crib, I'm 'bout to hop on a jet and just live in the sky ('Ky)"], "meaning": "It is clear that Yeat is no normal man, and this has been evidenced time and time again through his “flexation” of his lavish lifestyle. However, he takes it to the next level with this bar, making the diabolical claim that he no longer needs a homestead on the ground to reside in because he can simply inhabit a flying aircraft, also known as an airplane or a jetliner. This exemplifies his vast wealth, as it is copiously expensive to live your life in a private jet, especially when you consider the cost of items such as fuel in today’s economy!\n\nListed below is an example of a jet that Yeat might “hop into”:", "annotation_id": "25112585", "url": "https://genius.com/25112585", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Still countin", "line": "I’m on the Xan’ and the bean", "vault_lines": ["I'm on the Xan' and the bean"], "meaning": "In this line Noah Olivier Smith (b. February 26, 2000), better known by his stage name Yeat, means when he states the following “I’m on the Xan'” he indicates that he is using drugs or high on drugs, in this scenario he is high on “Xan” (Alprazolam, brand name being“Xanax”. ) The drug was not made for any recreational use. (for pleasure or for some other casual purpose or pastime, With the initial use to treat anxiety and panic disorders) Along with being high off of Xanax, Noah Olivier Smith is also “on” or high off “the bean” (A bean means ecstasy (MDMA) or a pill.)", "annotation_id": "25112043", "url": "https://genius.com/25112043", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Still countin", "line": "Luh wonky, luh wonky, I booted up, yeah (Booted up)", "vault_lines": ["Luh wonky, luh wonky, I booted up, yeah (Booted up)"], "meaning": "Yeat is addicted to substances such as lean and percocets. Whenever he is really high off of them, he refers to the feeling as being, “wonky.” A common phrase when off these drugs is “booted up” as you see here. And “luh” is just a filler word he uses to express himself at times. So in conclusion, Yeat is very high and activated off of drugs which most likely are some form of opioids.", "annotation_id": "25109672", "url": "https://genius.com/25109672", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Still countin", "line": "Some of my twizzys real deal crazy, they get you whacked (Whew-whew-whew)", "vault_lines": ["Some of my twizzys real deal crazy, they get you whacked (Whew-whew-whew)"], "meaning": "It has been said that the English language is one of the hardest to learn due to its many unorthodox quirks. For example, the word crazy is often used in reference to a person that is considered to be mentally deranged. However, this definition is not rigid, and Yeat capitalizes on this discrepancy. When Yeat depicts his “twizzys” as “real deal crazy”, you and I both know that he is not calling his fellow twizzys insane, but rather that they are ready and willing at the drop of a hat to use excessive firearm force if necessary to “drop an opp”. This is likely what Yeat is alluding to when he sings that his twizzys will indeed “get you whacked”(Yeat, Line 13), or in other words, murdered.\n\nListed below is a picture of Yeat with his “Twizzy”, SeptembersRich, with both rocking the esteemed turbans:", "annotation_id": "25106090", "url": "https://genius.com/25106090", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Still countin", "line": "Some of my twizzys real deal crazy, they get you whacked (Whew-whew-whew)", "vault_lines": ["Some of my twizzys real deal crazy, they get you whacked (Whew-whew-whew)"], "meaning": "It has been said that the English language is one of the hardest to learn due to its many unorthodox quirks. For example, the word “crazy” is often used in reference to a person that is considered to be mentally deranged. However, this definition is not rigid, and Yeat capitalizes on this discrepancy. When Yeat depicts his “twizzys” as “real deal crazy,” he’s not calling his fellow twizzys insane, but rather that they are ready and willing at the drop of a hat to use excessive firearm force if necessary to “drop an opp.” This is likely what Yeat is alluding to when he sings that his twizzys will indeed “get you whacked,” or in other words, murdered.\n\nListed below is a picture of Yeat with his “twizzy” SeptembersRich, with both rocking the esteemed turbans:", "annotation_id": "25106079", "url": "https://genius.com/25106079", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Still countin", "line": "Heard there’s a fountain of youth (You-youth), told ‘em to fill it with Wock’", "vault_lines": ["Heard there's a fountain of youth (You-youth), told 'em to fill it with Wock'"], "meaning": "When 16th-century Spanish explorer/adventurer, Juan Ponce de Leon, was trekking through what is now Florida, he allegedly stumbled upon what he believed to be a Fountain of Immortal Youth. I’m sure, however, that he could not have predicted a shoutout from rising star Noah Oliver “Yeat” Smith in his new single Still Countin, formerly known as Luh Crank. Yeat makes a masterful comparison between the purple drank in his cup and the liquid elixir fabled to give those who drink it immortality. Yeat may be justifying his Wock-addiction by making the grand claim that the lean he is sipping upon may be the key to preventing his aging process.\n\nOne artist’s depiction of the Fountain of Youth:", "annotation_id": "25106044", "url": "https://genius.com/25106044", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Still countin", "line": "Racks came in, I was still in the Tonka, ha (Ooh-ooh) Brats came in, they can’t fit in this Tonka, ha (Ooh) Racks came in, but I’m still in this Tonka, yeah (Ooh-ooh) Seven mil’ on the table and I’m still sayin’ Tonka, yeah (Ooh-ooh)", "vault_lines": ["Racks came in, I was still in the Tonka, ha (Ooh-ooh)"], "meaning": "Yeat been known for being addicted on saying the word “Tonka” on his songs which reffers to a GLE, Lamb Truck, Ranges, and other cars. The “Tonka” trend first started with Yeat doing a song called “Tonka” which was on his album “Alivë”. the song is what made yeat slowly become popular. Yeat also been saying Tonka before he got more rich.", "annotation_id": "25103312", "url": "https://genius.com/25103312", "state": "pending", "votes": 55, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Still countin", "line": "Yeah, I make too much cheese, I don’t want no dealings (With ya’)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I make too much cheese, I don't want no dealings (With ya')"], "meaning": "Cheese is a little slang for money which is used frequently in hip-hop. Yeat is saying he doesn’t want no dealings about money with him cause he’s too addicted to his own money.\n\nHe’s too rich to do dealings.\n\nYeat has a lyric in another song which also contains “cheese” in one of the lines:\n\nWe boutta get to the bread, yeah the cheese, yeah the miracle whip getting the things that let us on", "annotation_id": "25103229", "url": "https://genius.com/25103229", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Still countin", "line": "Luh crank, luh crank, luh crank (Ooh-ooh) Luh crank, luh crank, luh crank (Ooh-ooh, go)", "vault_lines": ["Luh crank, luh crank, luh crank (Ooh-ooh)"], "meaning": "“Luh crank” is a slang phrase coined by Yeat that he frequently uses in social media posts. The phrase consists of two components:\n\n  \n\"Luh,\" a multi-purpose term used interchangeably to mean \"love,\" \"lil,\" and \"the.\"  \n\"Crank,\" a slang term for a Percocet, a form of oxycodone medication used to treat pain.", "annotation_id": "25100554", "url": "https://genius.com/25100554", "state": "accepted", "votes": 50, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
