{"type": "song", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Slamm", "status": "done", "targets": 19, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Yeat-slamm-lyrics", "genius_title": "Slamm", "genius_id": 8844606, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Skufflr", "line": "Fuck this game, I decided I’m the chef", "vault_lines": ["Fuck this game, I decided I'm the chef (Swer-swerve, swerve)"], "meaning": "Calling himself “the chef” plays on the idea of cooking, slang for creating or performing extremely well.\n\nInstead of simply participating in “the game,” Yeat presents himself as the person preparing or controlling what’s being served. The metaphor reinforces his confidence that he creates his own style rather than following what everyone else is doing.", "annotation_id": "41613069", "url": "https://genius.com/41613069", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Skufflr", "line": "Hey, I want the Cloche if I want the watch", "vault_lines": ["Hey, I want the Cloche if I want the watch (Hey)"], "meaning": "Yeat appears to be referring to the Cloche de Cartier, one of Cartier’s more unconventional watch designs. “Cloche” means “bell” in French, referring to the model’s distinctive bell-shaped case. The design originated in Cartier’s early-20th-century watchmaking and has been released in limited editions.", "annotation_id": "41613064", "url": "https://genius.com/41613064", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Skufflr", "line": "We some of the greatest, some of the goats, and stole Mt. Rushmore", "vault_lines": ["We some of the greatest, some of the goats, and stole Mt. Rushmore (Let's go)"], "meaning": "“We some of the greatest, some of the goats, and stole Mt. Rushmore”\n\n“GOAT” stands for “Greatest Of All Time.” Yeat extends the idea of being among the greatest with the reference to Mount Rushmore, a monument depicting four U.S. presidents.\n\nRather than simply claiming a place on a hypothetical “Mount Rushmore” of great artists, Yeat exaggerates the flex by saying they “stole” Mount Rushmore itself, as though their greatness has allowed them to take ownership of the monument.\n\nThe Mount Rushmore reference also directly connects to the song’s title, “Rushmore.”", "annotation_id": "41613061", "url": "https://genius.com/41613061", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Skufflr", "line": "I did it myself, I did it my way, I don’t be in no rush no more", "vault_lines": ["I did it myself, I did it my way, I don't be in no rush no more (Hey)"], "meaning": "Yeat attributes his success to following his own approach rather than depending on other people.\n\n“I don’t be in no rush no more” also reflects the position that success has put him in: after reaching a level of financial security and recognition, he feels less pressure to hurry toward the next milestone.", "annotation_id": "41613055", "url": "https://genius.com/41613055", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Skufflr", "line": "I went and bought the GLE-ski, I don’t ride no bus no more", "vault_lines": ["I went and bought the GLE-ski, I don't ride no bus no more (Swerve), yeah"], "meaning": "“GLE-ski” is Yeat’s nickname for the Mercedes-Benz GLE, continuing his habit of adding “-ski” to words and luxury-car names.", "annotation_id": "41613054", "url": "https://genius.com/41613054", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Skufflr", "line": "I brought the blick outside today, we ain’t get in no scuffle", "vault_lines": ["I brought the blick outside today, we ain't get in no scuffle (Bop-bop), yeah"], "meaning": "“Blick” is slang for a firearm, while a “scuffle” is a physical fight.\n\nYeat contrasts the two by saying he came outside armed rather than expecting a fistfight. The bar emphasizes that any confrontation involving him could escalate beyond simply fighting with someone.", "annotation_id": "41613051", "url": "https://genius.com/41613051", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Skufflr", "line": "And they lie to themselves all day, they be in they bubble", "vault_lines": ["And they lie to themselves all day, they be in they bubble (Yeah, swerve)"], "meaning": "Being “in a bubble” means being isolated from reality or surrounded by beliefs that reinforce your own perspective. Yeat suggests these people lie to themselves so frequently that they have created their own version of reality, disconnected from what is actually happening around them.", "annotation_id": "41613049", "url": "https://genius.com/41613049", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Skufflr", "line": "I’m inside my bag today, I’m inside my duffle, bag", "vault_lines": ["I'm inside my bag today, I'm inside my duffle, bag (Bag)"], "meaning": "“In my bag” is a common expression for being locked in, performing at a high level, or focused on making money. Yeat turns the expression literal by following it with “inside my duffle bag,” tying it back to the Louis Vuitton duffel mentioned in the intro.", "annotation_id": "41613041", "url": "https://genius.com/41613041", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
