{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Shade", "line": "[Chorus]", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, fuck off, fuck off"], "meaning": "It is likely that Yeat was inspired by Charlie Puth’s song — Attention, as he sings with a very similar vocal flow as in the Attention:\n\nLyrics in “Attention”:\n\nYou’ve been runnin' ‘round, runnin’ ‘round, runnin’ ‘round\nThrowin’ that dirt all on my name\n\nLyrics in “Shade”:\n\nWasn’t up, wasn’t up, wasn’t up, wasn’t up, wasn’t up, wasn’t up\nI was broke, I was down, I was down bad, now I’m up, now I’m really up", "annotation_id": "33553291", "url": "https://genius.com/33553291", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Shade", "line": "I can see all between the lines that you said that you wouldn’t do And times that you said that you’d come, but you never do", "vault_lines": ["Yeah and it's sad that you said what you wouldn't do"], "meaning": "A drug-addled play on “read between the lines”. The person Yeat is addressing (possibly himself) is making no effort to curb his or her drug habit, continuing to snort lines despite telling Yeat they’ll stop. This person is also making no effort to honour their commitments to come support him. In short, they’re letting him down, and he can “read between the lines” of their behaviour that they aren’t really committed to him.", "annotation_id": "31883533", "url": "https://genius.com/31883533", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Shade", "line": "I don’t like to talk, I plead the fifth", "vault_lines": ["I don't like to talk, I plead the fifth"], "meaning": "Yeat is implying that people around him and his fanbase want him to speak, be public, but he doesn’t like talking so he pleads the fifth. “The fifth” refers to the United States' Fifth Amendment in the Bill of Rights. Giving people the option to not speak if it incriminates themselves. Yeat uses this as a way of saying he doesn’t have to speak.", "annotation_id": "30931315", "url": "https://genius.com/30931315", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Shade", "line": "Yeah, I was out sad (Out sad) in the Bentley truck", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I was out sad (Out sad) in the Bentley truck"], "meaning": "Back when Yeat was underground in the group called Slayworld in 2020 and early 2021, most people did not like his sound and said he was trash so he was left out sad from the rest of the group.\n\nOn the song 3AM Yeat says:\n\n“Yeah, they don’t like how I’m rich, they don’t like how I sound”", "annotation_id": "30928257", "url": "https://genius.com/30928257", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Shade", "line": "I just made the whole world my bitch", "vault_lines": ["I just made the whole world my bitch"], "meaning": "This could refer to the time Yeat rose to popularity around early 2020s from social media platforms like TikTok with songs like “Sorry Bout That”, “Swërved It”, “Monëy so big”, among others.", "annotation_id": "30911677", "url": "https://genius.com/30911677", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Shade", "line": "Yeah, and it’s sad that you said what you wouldn’t do (So sad) And it’s sad to look back at what you wouldn’t do", "vault_lines": ["And it's more than that"], "meaning": "In this line Yeat is saying SeptembersRich once told him that he was gonna quit on being addicted to lean and drugs so much, since he was highly addictive to it. But that proved to be wrong which eventually made him into a crash out\n\nhttps://youtu.be/vrE2GQQ8Njg?si=WCcraG4Gv9ycgLUY", "annotation_id": "30903970", "url": "https://genius.com/30903970", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Shade", "line": "And she called me, “Your Majesty”", "vault_lines": ["And she called me, \"Your Majesty\""], "meaning": "“Your Majesty” is a phrase used as a direct form of address of people of royalty. In this line Yeat is saying his girlfriend known as Symone treats him as a king.", "annotation_id": "30903845", "url": "https://genius.com/30903845", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Shade", "line": "I was broke, I was down, I was down bad, now I’m up, now I’m really up", "vault_lines": ["I was broke, I was down, I was down bad, now I'm up, now I'm really up"], "meaning": "Back when Yeat was underground, Yeat was very broke so he scammed to stay afloat which made him able to purchase a lot of expensive things.\n\nIn the song Free em Yeat says:\n\n“I was down bad in the basement it had roaches in it”\n\nWhich references that Yeat lived a bad lifestyle before his music took off.", "annotation_id": "30903705", "url": "https://genius.com/30903705", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Shade", "line": "And it’s sad, and it’s sad And it’s sad, and it’s sad (Ah) But I’m glad that you had your fun, you had your fun on it But I’m bad for the things I said and the things that I want on it How you mad that I got this money that I never even asked for? How you sad ’bout a slag? We wasn’t even up for it", "vault_lines": ["\"How could I make it up to you?\""], "meaning": "This line might be a reference to SeptembersRich who once was Yeat’s closest friend that switched up on him. Historically back when SeptembersRich and Yeat was underground, they were supposed to go up in the music scene together.\n\nOn the song Stayëd tha same Yeat says:\n\n“Yeah, I, I, I just walk in with the\nI, I, with the same gang\nI got rich with the same gang”\n\nBut SeptembersRich was jealous that Yeat was getting all the fame while fans were hating on him saying his music was trash, so he said some rude stuff behind Yeat’s back.", "annotation_id": "30903652", "url": "https://genius.com/30903652", "state": "pending", "votes": 27, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
