{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "I count money (Nuh-nuh-nuh, nuh-nuh-nuh, no) I count money (Nuh-nuh-nuh, nuh-nuh-nuh, no) I count money (Nuh-nuh-nuh, nuh-nuh-nuh, no)", "vault_lines": ["I count money (Nuh-nuh-nuh, nuh-nuh-nuh, no)"], "meaning": "Counting money is a common way to see your wealth.\n\n\nHe could be saying that he is so rich he counts money.", "annotation_id": "28596950", "url": "https://genius.com/28596950", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "Yeah, my diamonds glitter (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, my diamonds glitter (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Yeat’s diamonds shine so much they look as if they are glittering.", "annotation_id": "26168229", "url": "https://genius.com/26168229", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "I’m in high demand, got a lot of millions I got a high demand, I got all my millions, uh I’m in high demand, I got all my Minions", "vault_lines": ["I call my children my Minions"], "meaning": "Yeat’s phrase “I’m in high demand” originally stems from  his April 2022 song “Nëw turban.” It dropped as part of the deluxe version of Yeat’s second studio album, 2 Alivë (Geëk Pack).\n\nHis continued usage of the term, as well as his repetition of it in this track, implies that Yeat is continuing to receive ever-increasing offers and is getting paid more millions.\n\nhttps://genius.com/26172408", "annotation_id": "26172427", "url": "https://genius.com/26172427", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "I got diamonds on my body, I got every flavor", "vault_lines": ["I got diamonds on my body, I got every flavor"], "meaning": "Yeat has a vast collection of diamond jewelry, most notably in the form of diamond-encrusted watches from brands such as Rolex, Cartier, Audemars Piguet, and Hublot.\n\n\n\nPictured above: Yeat’s watch collection (via Instagram Stories).\n\n\n\nFurther, in early 2022, Yeat commissioned a multicolored “Tonka” pendant from Atlanta jewellers Icebox.\n\n\n\nPictured above: Renderings of Yeat’s forthcoming “Tonka” pendant (via Icebox).", "annotation_id": "26168852", "url": "https://genius.com/26168852", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "It’s bananas for the Minions and my pockets bread", "vault_lines": ["It's bananas for the Minions and my pockets bread"], "meaning": "Demonstrating his thoughtful side, Yeat has brought a selection of bananas for the Minions to enjoy.\n\nAccording to Despicable Me canon, bananas are the Minion’s favorite food, and one of the few words that are the same in both English and Minionese.\n\n\n\nUsing a food motif, Yeat juxtaposes the Minions' bananas with his own “bread,” which is a slang term for money.\n\n\n\nHere, “bananas” could be a double entendre, referring not only to the fruit but also to a “banana clip,” which is a nickname for a large-capacity magazine with a curved shape that resembles a banana. While the Minions typically use fart guns, Yeat may have upgraded their arsenal of weapons with automatic firearms.", "annotation_id": "26168766", "url": "https://genius.com/26168766", "state": "accepted", "votes": 71, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "I shop and then donate to charity (Woo, I donate it to charity)", "vault_lines": ["I shop and then donate to charity (Woo, I donate it to charity)"], "meaning": "Despite often perpetuating a tough-guy persona, here, Yeat demonstrates his thoughtful side, revealing that, once he’s finished with his designer clothes, he donates them to charity. In keeping with the song’s theme, this corresponds with the actions of Gru and the Minions in the Despicable Me films. Whilst Gru starts as a nefarious super-villain, committing heinous crimes across the world with the help of his Minions, he later has a change of heart, renouncing his villainous ways to become a hero.", "annotation_id": "26165225", "url": "https://genius.com/26165225", "state": "accepted", "votes": 99, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "I made a song for the Minions, uh How much they paid me? A million, uh", "vault_lines": ["I made a song for the Minions, uh"], "meaning": "On June 17, 2022, Cole Bennett, the founder and owner of multimedia company Lyrical Lemonade, revealed that he had been tasked by Illumination to make a trailer for the upcoming film Minions: The Rise of Gru, upon which he requested to use a song by his frequent collaborator, up-and-coming rapper Yeat, for the soundtrack. Here, Yeat claims that he was paid one million dollars for his contributions to the song, “Rich Minion.” As of the song’s release, this claim is unsubstantiated.", "annotation_id": "26165002", "url": "https://genius.com/26165002", "state": "pending", "votes": 107, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "Take a cranky, I be smacking the ceiling, uh", "vault_lines": ["Take a cranky, I be smacking the ceiling, uh"], "meaning": "After enduring a day full of life’s many hardships, Yeat unwinds by taking a “cranky,” which is a slang term for a Percocet, a form of oxycodone medication used to treat pain.\n\nYeat regularly raps about taking crankies. A notable example is his 2022 song “Gëek high,” on which he raps:\n\nFirst thing I did in the morning when I woke up, took a cranky, then I got to the junkie, yeah\n\n\n\nYeat’s description of the cranky’s effect, causing him to “smack the ceiling,” could be a reference to the Anti-Gravity Serum from Despicable Me, which causes those who drink it to repel Earth’s gravity and float upwards.", "annotation_id": "26164972", "url": "https://genius.com/26164972", "state": "pending", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "I call my Minion my children (Children) I call my children my Minions", "vault_lines": ["I call my Minion my children (Children)"], "meaning": "While Yeat’s music is usually filled with braggadocious lyrics, here, he throws caution to the wind, dropping the tough-guy facade to reveal a compassionate side. He embraces his vulnerability, sharing his intimate connection with the Minions, revealing that they are more than just hired help. Rather, they are like children to him. In a violent world where his life could be taken from him at a moment’s notice, Yeat embraces his fatherly love for the Minions.\n\nYeat could also be describing his die-hard fans as “Minions,” assuming a Gru-esque role in relation to his army of loyal supporters.", "annotation_id": "26164893", "url": "https://genius.com/26164893", "state": "pending", "votes": 114, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "I swerve in a Humvee (Nuh-nuh-nuh)", "vault_lines": ["I swerve in a Humvee (Nuh-nuh-nuh)"], "meaning": "Produced by AM General, the High Mobility Multipurpose Wheeled Vehicle, colloquially known as a “Humvee,” is the standard multipurpose vehicle of the United States Army. This military vehicle is seemingly an upgrade from Yeat’s infamous “Tonka” trucks.", "annotation_id": "26162973", "url": "https://genius.com/26162973", "state": "accepted", "votes": 62, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "I had to make me some sense (What?)", "vault_lines": ["I had to make me some sense (What?)"], "meaning": "Yeat is known for his unique lingo, with notable examples including “twizzy,” which refers to a friend, and “luh,” which is a replacement for “the.”\n\nMuch like the title Minions, many of Yeat’s words confuse listeners. Thus, Yeat demonstrates a sense of self-awareness, recognizing that some of his phrases might confuse his fans.\n\nThe ad-lib adds humor to the line, as “What?” is an expression that is often used to express confusion, which is ironic since Yeat has just expressed a desire to clarify himself.\n\n\n\nThis could also be a play on words, with “sense” sounding similar to “cents,” as Yeat spends a majority of the track rapping about the money he has made from collaborating with Illumination on “Rich Minion.”", "annotation_id": "26162511", "url": "https://genius.com/26162511", "state": "accepted", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "I just pulled up in a Tonka with all of the minions, we takin’ a picture (Ooh, ah-ooh)", "vault_lines": ["I just pulled up in a Tonka with all of the minions, we takin' a picture (Ooh, ah-ooh)"], "meaning": "While Yeat’s usual choice of Tonka is a Mercedes-Benz AMG GLE 63, here, he references the infamous Grumobile, a weaponized, rocket-powered vehicle used as the primary means of transport for Gru and the Minions.", "annotation_id": "26160029", "url": "https://genius.com/26160029", "state": "pending", "votes": 110, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "Yeah, I don’t like Vector (I don’t like— I don’t like Vector) Yeah, I don’t like Vector (Take ’em out)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I don't like Vector (I don't like— I don't like Vector)"], "meaning": "Furthering his affiliation with the Minions, Yeat expresses his disdain for Victor “Vector” Perkins, a notorious super villain and the antagonist of Despicable Me.\n\n\n\nPictured above: Vector from Despicable Me.\n\n\nOne of Vector’s most notable crimes is his theft of the Pyramid of Giza. Gru, the protagonist of Despicable Me, saw this as an attempt to dethrone him from his infamy, leading him to enact his long-time plans of stealing the moon. Gru assembled his army of Minions to assist in completing the egregious mission, further explaining the rivalry between Vector and the Minions.\n\n\n\nPictured above: Gru overlooking his army of Minions.", "annotation_id": "26142230", "url": "https://genius.com/26142230", "state": "accepted", "votes": 202, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "line": "Ha (Huh?), hey Mel, la bastichi La papaya, du la potato (Yeah, la potato, oh, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Ha (Huh?), hey Mel, la bastichi"], "meaning": "The Minions are small, yellow creatures that appear prominently in Illumination’s Despicable Me franchise. The Minions speak a unique language, containing sounds and inflections which are largely unintelligible but are often humorous to the films' projected younger audience.\n\nThese lines are sampled from a scene in Despicable Me 3:\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_RG8hoGMxKw", "annotation_id": "26112407", "url": "https://genius.com/26112407", "state": "accepted", "votes": 164, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rich Minion", "status": "done", "targets": 14, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Yeat-rich-minion-lyrics", "genius_title": "Rich Minion", "genius_id": 8114542, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rëal SPill", "line": "Yeah, I was on the sidelines before I was in the hills", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I was on the sidelines before I was in the hills"], "meaning": "Yeat reminisces about his life before the fame, reflecting on a time when he was “on the sidelines,” meaning that he was a nobody who felt overlooked and unimportant. In contrast, “in the hills” refers to his current lavish lifestyle, associating himself with celebrity culture and wealthy areas like Beverly Hills.", "annotation_id": "39648701", "url": "https://genius.com/39648701", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
