{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rackie Fitness", "line": "With these racks, you don’t got no bones, you a vegetarian", "vault_lines": ["With these racks, you don't got no bones, you a vegetarian"], "meaning": "Yeat is saying in this line that he has racks (money, typically referred in thousands) and then says “You don’t got no bones”. Bones can be used as a term for money, usually in dollars. He then goes on to say “you a vegetarian” Which he previously says “You don’t go no bones” and meat usually has bones in them.", "annotation_id": "28739153", "url": "https://genius.com/28739153", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Rackie Fitness", "line": "Got off the phone, I was with my twizzy, we was movin’ chickens", "vault_lines": ["Got off the phone, I was with my twizzy, we was movin' chickens"], "meaning": "In this line, Yeat is saying he was on the phone with his twizzy, a nickname that has a similar usage to twin or spider that originated from Yeat. Then yeat says, “we was movin' chickens”. Chicken is a term used for a kilogram or in this case kilograms of cocaine. Cocaine dealers originally started by referring to kilos of cocaine as birds, then switched to chickens.", "annotation_id": "28739131", "url": "https://genius.com/28739131", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
