{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Psychocainë", "line": "Yeah, I could sell you peace, but I could kill it too (Watch, watch, and they—)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I could sell you peace, but I could kill it too"], "meaning": "The line “I could sell you peace” aligns with the overarching theme of the Yeat’s presented god-like persona. By asserting the ability to provide peace, Yeat positions himself as a deity or figure with the power to bring a sense of tranquility and order to others' lives. This role goes hand in hand with the repeated claims of being a god throughout the song.\n\nHowever, the subsequent line, “but I could kill it too,” introduces a layer of complexity to his divine portrayal. It implies that Yeat, despite his god-like attributes, holds the capability to disrupt the peace he offers. This paradoxical nature aligns with the duality often associated with divine figures – the ability to bring both blessings and chaos.\n\nIn the context of the song’s overall theme, these lines contribute to a narrative of the Yeat’s self-perceived importance and influence. He presents a character who not only revels in his god-like status but also acknowledges the potential for both benevolent and destructive actions, adding depth to the portrayal of power and control.", "annotation_id": "30898564", "url": "https://genius.com/30898564", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Psychocainë", "line": "I could take a loss, I could take a loss I ain’t took a loss in so long, I forgot", "vault_lines": ["I could take a loss, I could take a loss"], "meaning": "Yeat sets a tone of resilience with the chorus by portraying himself as someone who is unaccustomed to setbacks.\n\nIn the context of the song, it sets the tone for a character who perceives setbacks as temporary or inconsequential, reinforcing an attitude of invincibility or unwavering confidence.", "annotation_id": "30898512", "url": "https://genius.com/30898512", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Psychocainë", "status": "done", "targets": 21, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Yeat-psychocaine-lyrics", "genius_title": "Psychocainë", "genius_id": 10060594, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Psycho CEO", "line": "I’m in 2093, where your life at? (Ah, ah, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I'm in 2093, where your life at? (Ah, ah, yeah)"], "meaning": "In this line, Yeat connects his music’s trademarks to the way it transcends his actual age.\n\nEven before the establishment of his record label Lyfestyle Corporation in September 2023, Yeat has made many references to life and death throughout his discography. This can be observed in album titles like 4L and AftërLyfe. (Lyfestylë also happens to be the name of track 8 in 2093 / track 9 in 2093 P2 and P3). By the time 2093 rolls around, many people worldwide will have died, and our memories of them will have been lost to time. In this line, Yeat implies that he’ll still be here in one way or another, most likely through his music.", "annotation_id": "31290637", "url": "https://genius.com/31290637", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Psycho CEO", "line": "Uh, we got G-Wagons, big money, big balls (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Uh, we got G-Wagons, big money, big balls (Yeah)"], "meaning": "The Mercedes Benz G-Wagon is a luxury SUV car model based on military off-roading vehicles. The 2024 model will run you upwards of $140,000, demonstrating Yeat’s wealth.\n\nYeat is no stranger to mentioning the G Wagon. He brings it up later on this album on the song Power Trip.\n\n\nYeat pictured with a black G-Wagon.", "annotation_id": "31008141", "url": "https://genius.com/31008141", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Psycho CEO", "line": "Wear your diamonds clean like me, but can you wife that? (Ah, ah, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Wear your diamonds clean like me, but can you wife that? (Ah, ah, yeah)"], "meaning": "Yeat is wearing flawless VVS diamonds and notices others wearing similar jewelry but he isn’t so sure their diamonds are real like his. He’s curious to know if you wipe them with a wet cloth that they might show their lack of validity.", "annotation_id": "30958034", "url": "https://genius.com/30958034", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Psycho CEO", "line": "Where my twizzies at? (Yeah) Where my motherfuckin’ army when I need that?", "vault_lines": ["Whеre my motherfuckin' army when I need that?"], "meaning": "Twizzy is a commonly used term created by Yeat as a nickname for someone close to you, like a twin. Yeat is asking where his people are when he needs them to give him support.", "annotation_id": "30957986", "url": "https://genius.com/30957986", "state": "pending", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
