{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "PUT IT ONG", "line": "BNYX", "vault_lines": ["Ride 'round this bitch, yeah, my money so tall, yeah, my money so tall, yeah, my money so mammoth (BNYX)"], "meaning": "Benjamin Saint Fort (born April 23, 1995), professionally known as BNYX®, (pronounced Benny X), is a record producer from Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. He is known for his production for Drake, Yeat, Lil Uzi Vert, Travis Scott, and Lancey Foux, among others.\n\nBNYX® acquired his pseudonym as a result of taking his nickname, Benny, and attaching it to the letter X, which he describes stands for “extreme,” a quality that he strives to achieve when creating music.\n\nIn 2021, BNYX® signed to record label and production group, Working on Dying.\n\nI-I-I’m working on dying, BNYX", "annotation_id": "41572269", "url": "https://genius.com/41572269", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "PUT IT ONG", "line": "Bitch, you gon’ come meet the gremlins", "vault_lines": ["If you think that you gon' get my money, lil' bitch, you gon' come meet the gremlins"], "meaning": "“The Gremlins” is Yeat’s inner circle, a tight-knit group of associates who move with him and handle anyone who crosses the line. The name draws from the 1984 horror comedy film Gremlins, directed by Joe Dante and produced by Steven Spielberg, in which small creatures transform into chaotic, destructive monsters when the rules are broken. The original gremlin mythology dates back to World War II RAF pilots who blamed inexplicable aircraft malfunctions on mischievous fictional creatures. The Irish Times\n\n\n\nThe threat is simple: if you try to take Yeat’s money, you won’t be dealing with him. You’ll be dealing with the Gremlins. People you don’t see coming, people who operate outside the rules, people who are far harder to reason with than the artist himself.", "annotation_id": "39961169", "url": "https://genius.com/39961169", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "PUT IT ONG", "line": "Look at us wrong and we empty the clip (Hold up, bitch)", "vault_lines": ["Look at us wrong and we empty the clip (Hold up, bitch)"], "meaning": "This line means that if someone shows any sign of disrespect, even a simple glare, yeat will shoot them until his firearm is out of ammo and empties the weapon’s clip (magazine).", "annotation_id": "36485775", "url": "https://genius.com/36485775", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "PUT IT ONG", "line": "I’m finna make all these Percs legal, bitch, yeah, the thirtieth amendment Yeah, the thirtieth amendment", "vault_lines": ["I'm finna make all these Percs legal, bitch, yeah, the thirtieth amendment"], "meaning": "Perc, which is short for Percocet, a oxycodone pill that commonly weighs 30 mg per pill, is a common prescription drug rappers like to flex having access to, for both consumption and distribution for profits.\n\nIn the US, where Yeat is from, an amendment is a formal revision or addition to the US Constitution, wherein the law and it’s rules are regulated, with a  popular example of the 1st one, which is broadly known as the (1st) amendment (that is) protecting freedom of speech in the US.\n\nThere’s 27 amendments in the USA, and Yeat skips over the 28th and the 29th, for the 30th, because he’s talking about making percocets legal, and as mentioned previously, the number 30 indicating the common dosis in mg of a percocet pill, corresponds to the number of this amendment, which is why Yeat deliberatly chose for “the thirtieth amendment.”", "annotation_id": "36468782", "url": "https://genius.com/36468782", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "PUT IT ONG", "line": "Boot up lil’ bitch in the sky, yeah, they call me Amelia", "vault_lines": ["Boot up lil' bitch in the sky, yeah, they call me Amelia"], "meaning": "Amelia Earhart was a famous female aviator (hence Yeat “booting up” in the sky), known for attempting to circumnavigate the world in 1937. She disappeared during her flight July 2nd, with mystery and ongoing speculation surrounding her sudden disappearance.", "annotation_id": "36428868", "url": "https://genius.com/36428868", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
