{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Not Tha Samë", "line": "Watch what you say or what you do or what you get into", "vault_lines": ["Watch what you say or what you do or what you get into"], "meaning": "Following the prior lyric, questioning what you’re doing and working to achieve, Yeat gives out simple, but greatly undervalued life advice. Take great caution and consider all possible outcomes when considering your free will in speech and action. Many unconsciously make in the moment decisions in absence of a second thought. Consider how one action can change your life in seemingly endless outcomes. When watching what you say is referred, this can be interpreted as choosing your words carefully, understanding the implications, maintaining composure, and to not respond out of emotion.", "annotation_id": "38477411", "url": "https://genius.com/38477411", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Not Tha Samë", "line": "Always gon’ do what I want, you couldn’t do that if you wanted to", "vault_lines": ["Always gon' do what I want, you couldn't do that if you wanted to"], "meaning": "Yeat callin out broke people tellin em they don’t got time to do what they really want cuz they have to work, unlike yeat who already rich", "annotation_id": "37387225", "url": "https://genius.com/37387225", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Not Tha Samë", "line": "Yeah, your girl pulled up on me, she’s a creep, she wanna eat me (Huh)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, your girl pulled up on me, she's a creep, she wanna eat me (Huh)"], "meaning": "Yeat is saying that someone’s girl is pulling up on him to do extra curricular activities they won’t be to happy of", "annotation_id": "31402733", "url": "https://genius.com/31402733", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Not Tha Samë", "line": "I be high as hell but I be on my business too (My business)", "vault_lines": ["I be high as hеll but I be on my business too (My business)"], "meaning": "Yeat here is saying that he stays high off of narcotics while handling his business at the same time", "annotation_id": "26464581", "url": "https://genius.com/26464581", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Not Tha Samë", "line": "Heard they bitin’ swag, yeah, we call them barracudas", "vault_lines": ["Heard they bitin' swag, yeah, we call them barracudas"], "meaning": "To bite swag, is to hop on a very popular trend made by another successful person in hope of receiving that same success, Yeat implores this slang to all the copycats who tried to replicate Yeat’s iconic wordplay and/or flow.", "annotation_id": "31398155", "url": "https://genius.com/31398155", "state": "pending", "votes": -7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Not Tha Samë", "line": "I’m high with the gods, bitch, I just chill with Buddha", "vault_lines": ["I'm high with the gods, bitch, I just chill with Buddha"], "meaning": "Yeat say he is so high he can talk with Buddha", "annotation_id": "31023541", "url": "https://genius.com/31023541", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Not Tha Samë", "line": "You really be askin’ for the world, then show it what you do It’s not that complicated (No, no) Yeah, just do what you do, don’t need to complicate it", "vault_lines": ["You really be askin' for the world, then show it what you do"], "meaning": "Yeat recognizes that we all have desires. He is encouraging us as listeners to put in effort and maintain consistency rather than remain paralyzed by overthinking.", "annotation_id": "30823238", "url": "https://genius.com/30823238", "state": "pending", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Not Tha Samë", "line": "They said the wave wouldn’t make it, bitch, I’m living proof", "vault_lines": ["They said the wave wouldn't make it, bitch, I'm living proof"], "meaning": "Basically Yeat is saying the new wave of music had people doubting the new artists success, but Yeat proved them wrong by growing at a massive rate in a matter of 2 years.", "annotation_id": "27510870", "url": "https://genius.com/27510870", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Not Tha Samë", "line": "My money strong, your shit weak, yeah", "vault_lines": ["My money strong, your shit weak, yeah"], "meaning": "In this verse, Yeat plays with the pronunciation of the word “weak”, identical to that of the rapper and producer Autumn!’s nickname , “wick”. Thus he advocates against Benjamin C. Phillips Jr., suggesting that his personal assets are neither as many nor as lustful as Yeat’s, continuing his feud with him after the first preview of “Portrayal!”.\nFurthermore, it should be noted that Yeat is using the same beat for Autumn!’s song “WANTS!”, produced by Clibbo and Fallwood, and titled this piece similarly to Autumn!’s; “Not tha samë” (“WANTS!” stands for “We Are Not The Same”).", "annotation_id": "24627769", "url": "https://genius.com/24627769", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Not Tha Samë", "line": "But now that I got rich, I wish I never made it, hey", "vault_lines": ["But now that I got rich, I wish I never made it, hey"], "meaning": "Yeat takes more of a direct attack against greed and materialistic wealth in the final words of his verse. He states that he is now extremely wealthy, but simultaneously wishes he never acquired as much money as he did. Money and the quest for it can often cloud an individual’s brain, making them forget other aspects of their life that should remain front and center, such as appreciation of nature and self. But once one generates the aforementioned money, they may realize that money alone does not garner happiness or fulfillment. It is rather the simple appreciation for humanity and nature, especially the self as an individual, that renders true satisfaction possible.", "annotation_id": "24474005", "url": "https://genius.com/24474005", "state": "accepted", "votes": 37, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Not Tha Samë", "line": "Is it the drugs or it’s the money, what got into you? (Yeah, what?)", "vault_lines": ["Is it the drugs or it's the money, what got into you? (Yeah, what?)"], "meaning": "This bar is structured as a question, and it addresses the transcendental concern with the human tendency to live life based on materialistic luxuries that skew judgement. Both money and drugs are materialistic things that our society places high value upon, and people often get lost pursuing money or popping pills that they cannot live their lives as one with nature and themselves without being distracted by addictions. In this bar, Yeat is asking someone which worldly vice they succumbed to. Once artificial goals seep into one’s mind, it is often hard to readjust your mindset to become rationally in line with transcendentalism.", "annotation_id": "24473987", "url": "https://genius.com/24473987", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Not Tha Samë", "line": "I heard that you want this lifestyle but do it want you?", "vault_lines": ["I heard that you want this lifestyle but do it want you?"], "meaning": "This lyric can be interpreted as a question being stated by a transcendentalist. It’s a message to people who aren’t yet living a transcendental lifestyle, but maybe wish they were. Especially prominent is the transcendental idea of oversoul that we as humans contain earth, and earth contains us. This pertains specifically to the part of the lyric that states “but do it want you?”. Earth takes care of us, and thus it is our responsibility to care for and sustain Earth. So a person might desire to be a transcendentalist, but not be fully appreciative of nature or accepting of nature in the same capacity they are with themselves. You might aspire to be one with nature, but you must accept yourself first before the two can fully merge together.", "annotation_id": "24473973", "url": "https://genius.com/24473973", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
