{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Mëan feen", "line": "Matter of fact, you pray for the ceilin’ when you’re broke and teasin’ If they catch me, I’ma break it up and break my fuckin’ Visa", "vault_lines": ["Matter of fact, you pray for the cеilin' when you're broke and teasin'"], "meaning": "This lyric reflects the speaker’s mindset when facing financial difficulties. When someone is struggling financially (“broke and teasin'”), they often pray for help or a breakthrough. The line “pray for the ceilin'” symbolizes this desire for relief from financial constraints. However, the speaker suggests that if they are caught in a difficult situation, they would rather take drastic measures, such as breaking their credit card (“break my fuckin' Visa”), than passively wait for a solution. This highlights a sense of urgency and willingness to take action, even if it means resorting to extreme measures.", "annotation_id": "31092134", "url": "https://genius.com/31092134", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Mëan feen", "line": "Sippin’ lean, I’m drinkin’ track’, the purple beat the fizzy", "vault_lines": ["Sippin' lean, I'm drinkin' track', the purple beat the fizzy"], "meaning": "He starts off the bar by lettin u know bluntly that he “sippin lean”, then says it again but just using the word “track” which is uncommonly known as another way to say lean. Then he says “The purple beat the fizzy…”\n\nMeaning the race 🏁 to the bottom of the bottle he pouring up lean in, is won by “the purple” & it beats “the fizzy” since the lil bubbles stay up top. 🚜🐐", "annotation_id": "30808416", "url": "https://genius.com/30808416", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
