{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Mysëlf", "line": "Got ’em copy the trend like they me (Ah)", "vault_lines": ["Got 'em copy the trend like they me (Ah)"], "meaning": "Yeat is talking about his online fan base that copied his style. A lot of people on TikTok were making videos showcasing his ‘turban’ and his clothing, trying to look like him.", "annotation_id": "27923398", "url": "https://genius.com/27923398", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Mysëlf", "line": "I’m talkin’ ’bout these damages, from the drugs", "vault_lines": ["I'm talkin' 'bout these damages, from the drugs"], "meaning": "Yeat is meaning that his usage of substances is bringing damage to his life and possible his mentality. He also reflects about his influence on his fans and mentions here about his lyrics involving references to such substances, which could be damaging to his listeners, and he does not want to bring that on anyone, as he wants to make music to uplift others.", "annotation_id": "27897112", "url": "https://genius.com/27897112", "state": "pending", "votes": 35, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Mysëlf", "line": "Not even myself (Myself, yeah, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Not even myself (Myself, yeah, yeah)"], "meaning": "As a rapper, Yeat may be conscious of projecting an image of strength and resilience to his audience, and may view seeking help as a sign of weakness or vulnerability.\n\nThis statement suggests that Yeat is aware of the negative impact that drug use has had on his life, and he may be motivated to make a change on his own terms. By emphasizing his ability to “live with himself,” Yeat may be communicating a sense of personal accountability and determination to overcome his addiction without external support.", "annotation_id": "27890847", "url": "https://genius.com/27890847", "state": "pending", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Mysëlf", "line": "I don’t know how to feel, ah", "vault_lines": ["I don't know how to feel, ah"], "meaning": "This statement suggests that Yeat recognizes the negative impact that drug use has had on his emotional well-being, and he may feel lost or unsure about how to navigate his emotions without the aid of drugs.\n\nFurthermore, Yeat could imply that he doesn’t know what to do by conveying a sense of confusion and uncertainty in his words and actions. He may express a desire to stop using drugs but feel overwhelmed by the challenge of doing so, or he may feel unsure about where to turn for help and support. By acknowledging his own uncertainty and vulnerability, Yeat may be signaling a need for guidance and support from those around him in his journey towards recovery.", "annotation_id": "27890836", "url": "https://genius.com/27890836", "state": "pending", "votes": 45, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Mysëlf", "line": "I just feel like red rum", "vault_lines": ["I just feel like red rum"], "meaning": "This lyric is a reference to the movie ‘The Shining’. Specifically to the phrase that Danny writes on the door, being ‘murder’ backwards. This could be Yeat trying to say he feels he’s going insane, which would match the introspective and mellow tone of the song. Alternatively he could be saying he feels connected to the supernatrual, like Danny in The Shining, which is supported by the line “I just called the devil”", "annotation_id": "27878509", "url": "https://genius.com/27878509", "state": "pending", "votes": 34, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
