{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My wrist", "line": "Had all my money to fuck up, nineteen Ms, bitch, I’m the boss (Ooh, ooh, ooh, ooh, ooh-ooh)", "vault_lines": ["Had all my money to fuck up, nineteen Ms, bitch, I'm the boss (Ooh, ooh, ooh, ooh, ooh-ooh)"], "meaning": "The lyrics suggest that the speaker had a significant amount of money at a young age, implying financial success or influence.\n\nThe phrase “nineteen Ms” could refer to having millions of dollars at the age of 19, emphasizing the speaker’s wealth and success at a young age.\n\nThe line “bitch, I’m the boss” conveys a sense of confidence and power, implying that the speaker is in control and dominant in their endeavors.", "annotation_id": "28988136", "url": "https://genius.com/28988136", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My wrist", "line": "I was on hype (Hype), Travis Scott, I might (Might)", "vault_lines": ["I was on hype (Hype), Travis Scott, I might (Might)"], "meaning": "Young Thug is referencing to Travis Scott’s wordplay chorus on their previous hit collaboration, “FRANCHISE”:\n\nYeah, call up Hype Williams for the hype, please\n\nHype Williams is a Queens director who has worked with numerous artists, including A Boogie wit Da Hoodie and Young Thug’s music video, “Might Not Give Up”.\n\n\n\nClip of Young Thug & A Boogie Wit da Hoodie from Williams' “Might Not Give Up” video.", "annotation_id": "28780565", "url": "https://genius.com/28780565", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
