{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "Let’s ride with the gun", "vault_lines": ["Fuck it, let's ride with the gun"], "meaning": "“Ride with the gun” possibly means literally riding with a gun or a metaphor for a reckless lifestyle, as well as possibly alluding back to “FOREVER AGAIN“ on LYFESTYLE. “And I’m geeking, I ride with the gun”", "annotation_id": "39235718", "url": "https://genius.com/39235718", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "I went platinum, I went platinum bands, uh (My way)", "vault_lines": ["I went platinum, I went platinum bands, uh (My way)"], "meaning": "At the launch date of ADL, Yeat has 5 RIAA certified platinum songs.", "annotation_id": "39164649", "url": "https://genius.com/39164649", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "I went platinum, I want platinum bands, uh (My way)", "vault_lines": ["I went platinum, I want platinum bands, uh (My way)"], "meaning": "At the launch date of ADL, Yeat has 5 RIAA certified platinum songs.", "annotation_id": "39164632", "url": "https://genius.com/39164632", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "Bad bitch, she want me, they choosin’, baby You gotta take your time, yeah You gotta make up your mind, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Bad bitch, she want me, they choosin', baby"], "meaning": "These three lines seem to suggest Yeat is stuck and wanting a girl to make a choice of being with him or not. He is stating that there are women who desire him, but it is not bragging, it is a plea: he wants to know if this girl is in or not with him, thematically resonating with A Dangerous Lyfe/Love. A life of hoes, or love with her.\n\nThe second and third lines seem to be a little contradictory: one offers time for a decision to be made, the other pressures said decision to be reached. However, these could also be interpreted as plenty of time being offered in order to enable a decision to be made.\n\n“You cannot take your time” might be what he actually says, which would make much more sense structurally, as it would also make sense considering the next line of “fuck it” – he’s pleading for a choice to be made, and then just throwing his hands up and saying “fuck it, whatever”, which would thematically fit with the struggle of the song.", "annotation_id": "39159528", "url": "https://genius.com/39159528", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "They pushed me into the well so they could Drink, drink, drink, sink They need to see to believe, so I Sink, sink, sink, sink (Girl, you, girl, you, girl, you, girl, you, girl, you)", "vault_lines": ["They pushed me into the well so they could"], "meaning": "One of the more despondent songs off the album, Julia’s cooing to guitar strums set the tone of the song. Referencing “they”, this group are stated to figuratively force Yeat into something for their own benefit – in this case a “well” so that they may “drink” from it. Further, they need proof of the job being finished, and so Yeat “sinks”.\n\nAllegorically, the remainder of the song sees Yeat struggle and have several back-and-forth moments with things that have him “sideways”, which give new meaning to this into – the “they” now seems to be internal manifestations coming to the surface and trying to drown/“sink” a Yeat who is trying to hang on.\n\nFinally, the “girl, you—” four-count loop reveals a girl, one of these internal struggles. Sonically, the loop makes it sound like he’s struggling to put what he feels into words – for it has him “sideways”.", "annotation_id": "39159353", "url": "https://genius.com/39159353", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "Every Sunday, you call up your friends to tell me somethin’ (My way)", "vault_lines": ["Every Sunday, you call up your friends to tell me somethin' (My way)"], "meaning": "Might be a nod to his 2021 track “Every Sunday”.", "annotation_id": "39151322", "url": "https://genius.com/39151322", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "My way, pack got me sideways Pack got me sideways, pack got me sideways", "vault_lines": ["My way, pack got me sideways"], "meaning": "Yeat’s cigarette pack is hitting good. The fact he mentions it three times may hint at the fact he is addicted to them.\n\nThe track after, he mention’s he is a “nicotine fiend”.\nhttps://genius.com/39061422", "annotation_id": "39150739", "url": "https://genius.com/39150739", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "(Missing you for life)", "vault_lines": ["They pushed me into the well so they could"], "meaning": "This line introduces a rare moment of emotional vulnerability. Despite the dominant tone of control and excess, it hints at lingering attachment, showing that Yeat’s relationships still affect him beneath the surface.", "annotation_id": "39149306", "url": "https://genius.com/39149306", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "Uh, I’m in the middle of the money like sandwich", "vault_lines": ["Uh, I'm in the middle of the money like sandwich"], "meaning": "Yeat describes himself as surrounded by wealth on all sides, emphasizing total immersion in money. However, this also suggests being trapped within it, unable to step outside the lifestyle.", "annotation_id": "39149305", "url": "https://genius.com/39149305", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "Had me burn another page outta that book and act like nothin’ happened", "vault_lines": ["Had me burn another page outta that book and act like nothin' happened"], "meaning": "Burning a page suggests erasing part of his past or avoiding consequences. Instead of learning from experience, Yeat chooses to remove it entirely, continuing the cycle without reflection.", "annotation_id": "39149300", "url": "https://genius.com/39149300", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "I might be out my mind, I might be hallucinatin’", "vault_lines": ["I might be out my mind, I might be hallucinatin'"], "meaning": "Yeat openly acknowledges a loss of mental clarity, likely influenced by drugs and his lifestyle. This line directly contradicts his earlier claims of control, reinforcing the album’s theme of illusion versus reality.", "annotation_id": "39149295", "url": "https://genius.com/39149295", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "Oh, we do it my way or we don’t do it at all, huh We do it my way or we don’t do it at all, huh", "vault_lines": ["Oh, we do it my way or we don't do it at all, huh"], "meaning": "Yeat asserts dominance and control, presenting his way as the only option. However, within the context of ADL, this insistence feels like overcompensation, as his life increasingly reflects instability rather than control.", "annotation_id": "39149292", "url": "https://genius.com/39149292", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "line": "Money got me sideways Life got me sideways", "vault_lines": ["My way, pack got me sideways"], "meaning": "“Sideways” suggests imbalance rather than forward progress. Despite his success, Yeat feels mentally and emotionally off-center, implying that money and lifestyle have disrupted his sense of direction instead of stabilizing it.", "annotation_id": "39149282", "url": "https://genius.com/39149282", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Way", "status": "done", "targets": 13, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Yeat-and-julia-wolf-my-way-lyrics", "genius_title": "My Way", "genius_id": 13307630, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Time", "line": "Number (N)ine, Maison Margiela, my jeans Chrome Hearts Supply the crosses, all my genes got cold hearts", "vault_lines": ["Number (N)ine, Maison Margiela, my jeans Chrome Hearts"], "meaning": "In this bar, Yeat uses the physical appearance of clothing to make a clever triple entendre.\n\nIn the first highlighted line, yeat refers to “Number (N)ine,” which is a Chinese street wear brand. He then goes on to list Masion Margiela and Chrome Heart jeans, signifying a theme of dark, avant garde attire.\n\nThe wordplay, however, ensues in the second line. He says “apply the crosses,” which is a callback to the first line, about his Chrome Heart jeans, which feature crosses in their stitching. He then goes on to do a play on words, saying “all my genes”, which is a homophone for “jeans.” He ends it off by noting that his coldness is fostered into his genes.\n\nThis is a triple entendre because the second line starts off with “Apply the crosses,” which is actually a play on words because genes “cross over” during meosis to create new combinations of alleles to make a unique sperm or egg.\n\nbasically, yeat’s the greatest lyricist ever", "annotation_id": "39204488", "url": "https://genius.com/39204488", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "My Time", "line": "And I’m back on the bends, on a bad bend", "vault_lines": ["And I'm back on the bends, on a bad bend"], "meaning": "Interpolation of his track “Bad bënd / DëMON”:\n\nI’m on a bad bend on these pills, man, and this shit don’t stop (I’m on a bad bend)", "annotation_id": "39191274", "url": "https://genius.com/39191274", "state": "accepted", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
