{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Money Twërk", "line": "Balenciaga my sweatsuit", "vault_lines": ["Take the bitch to Margiela, shе go (Go)"], "meaning": "Yeat has so much money that he can use top tier luxury brands as swatsuit", "annotation_id": "35503828", "url": "https://genius.com/35503828", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Money Twërk", "line": "Fuck off the wave, you can’t ride in my boat (Skrrt)", "vault_lines": ["Fuck off the wave, you can't ride in my boat (Skrrt)"], "meaning": "Here, Yeat is gatekeeping his “wave” — a term in rap culture for style, sound, or unique energy. He’s basically saying you can’t copy me, you can’t hang with me, and you’re definitely not moving how I move.\n\nThe “boat” metaphor extends the wave idea — Yeat’s on his own journey, on his own level, and others are either not invited or just not capable of keeping up. The “skrrt” adds that signature Yeat flair, like he’s swerving off while leaving the biters behind.\n\nIt’s braggadocious, confident, and dismissive — all classic Yeat energy. He’s putting a boundary around his originality and letting everyone know: you’re not part of this motion.", "annotation_id": "35237565", "url": "https://genius.com/35237565", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Money Twërk", "line": "Color of the jeans, I buy Trueys and Ksubis (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Color of the jeans, I buy Trueys and Ksubis (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Yeat is flexing his designer taste with this line, name-dropping two high-end denim brands: True Religion (aka Trueys) and Ksubi (pronounced “soobie”). Both are known for their bold stitching, premium fits, and being fashion statements, especially in hip-hop and streetwear culture.\n\nBy mentioning the “color of the jeans,” he might be alluding to how fresh or standout his fits are — possibly referencing unique washes, dyes, or even color-coordinated outfits. It’s Yeat being Yeat: casually name-dropping expensive brands while keeping it trippy and melodic.\n\nIt’s a subtle flex wrapped in a catchy bounce.\n\nhttps://images.app.goo.gl/yy72Yd7xUtWWuerS8", "annotation_id": "35237544", "url": "https://genius.com/35237544", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Money Twërk", "line": "Color of the jeans, I buy Trueys and Ksubis (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Color of the jeans, I buy Trueys and Ksubis (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Yeat is flexing his designer taste with this line, name-dropping two high-end denim brands: True Religion (aka Trueys) and Ksubi (pronounced “soobie”). Both are known for their bold stitching, premium fits, and being fashion statements, especially in hip-hop and streetwear culture.\n\nBy mentioning the “color of the jeans,” he might be alluding to how fresh or standout his fits are — possibly referencing unique washes, dyes, or even color-coordinated outfits. It’s Yeat being Yeat: casually name-dropping expensive brands while keeping it trippy and melodic.\n\nIt’s a subtle flex wrapped in a catchy bounce.\n\nhttps://images.app.goo.gl/yy72Yd7xUtWWuerS8", "annotation_id": "35237523", "url": "https://genius.com/35237523", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
