{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Made It On Our Own", "line": "(I-I-I-I-I)", "vault_lines": ["Made it on our own (I-I-I-I-I)"], "meaning": "Vocal chop of the beginning of the Working on Dying tag. Usually, BNYX uses this variant of the tag but in this song its used by Mathaius Young as being apart of BNYX’s Dataclub producer and music publishing collective.", "annotation_id": "38962649", "url": "https://genius.com/38962649", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Made It On Our Own", "line": "Oh, we made it, oh, we made it (We made it on our own) Oh, we made this life together, so, we made it (We made it on our own) Crocodile leather patches, baby, oh (We made it on our own) Oh, we made it, uh, now my vision fa— (We made it on our— I-I-I)", "vault_lines": ["We made it on our own, uh"], "meaning": "oh vey made it", "annotation_id": "38884889", "url": "https://genius.com/38884889", "state": "pending", "votes": -16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Made It On Our Own", "line": "Bitch, I made it on my own I ain’t need to sign no clones", "vault_lines": ["Made it on our own (I-I-I-I-I)"], "meaning": "This is a possible diss towards rapper Playboi Carti, and his Opium and YVL collectives. Yeat is saying that his mainstream success is something that he did by himself, without needing to “sign no clones”.\n\nCarti’s Opium and YVL has artists such as Destroy Lonely, Ken Carson, HXG, and ApolloRed1, who use distorted rage sounds inspired from Carti’s influential “Whole Lotta Red”, which is still making major waves 5 years later.\n\nThis is not the first diss between Yeat and Opium, as this beef stems from all the way back in early 2022, when Yeat was previewing a song with Ken Carson, entitled “Gëek High”. The song dropped shortly after, as track 17, on Yeat’s second studio album, called “2 Alivë”.\n\nThe original preview/snippet showcased a finished Yeat verse, with what sounded like a badly mixed Ken Carson verse. Opium producer lil 88 took to twitter upon hearing this and posted a picture of some lyrics from Ken Carson’s song “Me N My Kup”.\n\nI don’t fuck with these niggas like they got a disease\n\nhttps://www.reddit.com/r/playboicarti/comments/1c0vhli/lil88_dissing_yeat_confirmed/\n\nThis reddit post confirms lil 88’s true intentions, as well as showing the tweet that was mentioned earlier.\n\nIt also shows the fact that Opium members Ken Carson and Destroy Lonely liked the post, suggesting that they sided with lil 88.\n\nOther disses include Ken Carson dissing Don Toliver and Yeat performing together at the Lyrical Lemonade Summer Smash 2025, where Ken mentioned something about duos on his Instagram story. This turned into a back and forth between Don and Ken via Instagram stories, with Ken Carson going as far as saying\n\nU the worst thing that happened to your BM. Pussy ahh\n\n(BM stands for baby mama, who, in this case, is Kali Uchis, prominent Latinopop and RnB singer)\n\nAs of recently, Don Toliver dropped his highly anticipated “OCTANE”, with guaranteed features from Yeat. Fans speculated about a Carti verse, but an alleged source said that Opium would not show up on the album because Yeat was featured.\n\nSpeculation continues on whether Opium and Yeat will ever set their differences aside and collaborate again.\n\nP.S. I deleted my old annotation on this line because it was 100% AI generated.\nAlso, links and other media will be posted in the following days. Open to any suggestions or comments. This postscript will be deleted once all media and links have been added.", "annotation_id": "38838340", "url": "https://genius.com/38838340", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Made It On Our Own", "line": "[Outro] We’re gonna get the chopper on ya, lad You’ve smoked it, it does one, innit You’re not gonna get caught Get the tunes on, someone get the tune—", "vault_lines": ["We're gonna get the chopper on ya, lad"], "meaning": "The outro for “Made It On Our Own” samples this video of Trent Alexander-Arnold and Curtis Jones giving Alexis Mac Allister a tour of Liverpool, eventually getting into a high speed chase.", "annotation_id": "38816815", "url": "https://genius.com/38816815", "state": "accepted", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
