{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Mad bout that", "line": "Yeah, bitch, I just went, ran it up, bitch, I went bonkers", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, bitch, I just went, ran it up, bitch, I went bonkers"], "meaning": "Yeat further expresses his madness and agitation through the repetition of ‘bitch’ in this sentence. Furthermore, Yeat conveys that he ‘ran it up’ which possibly equates to him increasing his net worth and his profits, as ‘running it up’ can mean increasing a number, the number in this context being the amount of $ (USD(US dollars)) that Yeat is currently in possession of. Additionally, Yeat uses a common British slang, ‘bonkers’ as a means of how effectively and wildly his funds increased, as ‘bonkers’ can mean ‘crazy’ or ‘insane’, and is often used as a method of describing behaviour, for example, “That child is acting bonkers”. Therefore, with the usage of ‘bonkers’, Yeat conveys that his financial status improved at an ‘insane’ and unbelievable rate.", "annotation_id": "28627294", "url": "https://genius.com/28627294", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
