{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Lose Control", "line": "Sweet freedom whispered in my ear You’re a butterfly And butterflies are free to fly Fly away, high away, fly, fly Ooh Ooh", "vault_lines": ["Sweet freedom whispered in my ear"], "meaning": "The intro to Yeat’s “Lose Control” samples “Someone Saved My Life Tonight” by Elton John, originally released in 1975.\n\nYou can find where the song was sampled here:\nhttps://youtu.be/A-2vN3YJ29k?si=SmXSDgljh5ldy264&t=82", "annotation_id": "39142546", "url": "https://genius.com/39142546", "state": "accepted", "votes": 11, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Lose Control", "line": "Ooh, woah, we was in the whip, drivin’ down the coast Yeah, I just passed the wheel to God, told God, “Yeah, I’ma lose control” (Lose control)", "vault_lines": ["Ooh, woah, we was in the whip, drivin' down the coast"], "meaning": "Yeat is setting a whole scene here driving down the coast is a vibe, but then he hits us with the line about passing the wheel to God. It’s Yeat saying he’s done trying to manage his life and is just letting fate take over. Yeat is playing on the “Jesus, take the wheel” saying, but instead of asking for help to stay safe, he’s giving up control so he can actually let go. It shows his “I don’t care” mentality toward his life and fame. He’s reached a point where he’s so successful or too far gone that he’d rather just let God take over while he embraces the chaos.", "annotation_id": "38857827", "url": "https://genius.com/38857827", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Lose Control", "line": "I’ma smoke it up now, I disappear", "vault_lines": ["I'ma smoke it up now, I disappear"], "meaning": "Even though Yeat’s off the pills now for ADL, he’s still California sober and still smokes. The “disappear” part is about how he stays out of the way and doesn’t chase clout. He just gets high, does his own thing, and goes ghost, until he drops.", "annotation_id": "38857776", "url": "https://genius.com/38857776", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Lose Control", "line": "Still, we was outside poppin’ pills, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Still, we was outside poppin' pills, yeah"], "meaning": "Yeat said in his recent interviews for ADL that he’s sober now and off the percs, so he’s basically looking back at his old lyfestyle. It’s like he’s reminiscing on the Up 2 Më/2 Alivë eras. He’s acknowledging that even though he’s moved on to this new sound and a cleaner life, and that the older lyfestyle is still a part of his history.", "annotation_id": "38857745", "url": "https://genius.com/38857745", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Lose Control", "status": "done", "targets": 4, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Yeat-and-elton-john-lose-control-lyrics", "genius_title": "Lose Control", "genius_id": 12224698, "source": "api"}
