{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "LYING 5 FUN", "line": "We don’t do old money, only do new money", "vault_lines": ["We don't do old money, only do new money"], "meaning": "Old money refers to money that is given not earned, new money is money that is newly earned. (In the book “The Great Gatsby” old money is money from someone that had so much money it was passed down to family, generational money. New money is money newly earned and not passed down).", "annotation_id": "39724914", "url": "https://genius.com/39724914", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "LYING 5 FUN", "line": "Slap that bitch from the back, I’m a dog, she holdin’ this pound", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, slap that bitch from the back, I'm a dog, she holdin' this pound"], "meaning": "Yeat is fornicating with a woman, describing himself as a dog because he is hitting her from the back, also known as “doggy style”. The meaning of “holdin' this pound” relates to the dog mention, as shelters for stray dogs are referred as pounds, while also being slang for sex (pounding). This is a creative double meaning, hitting her from the back like a dog, while she is taking the pound.", "annotation_id": "39291978", "url": "https://genius.com/39291978", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "LYING 5 FUN", "line": "Ooh, ah-ah", "vault_lines": ["(Ooh, ah, woah, woah) Sittin' outside, sittin' outside, bitch"], "meaning": "he is surely done now", "annotation_id": "35457928", "url": "https://genius.com/35457928", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "LYING 5 FUN", "line": "You must have thought I was—, you must have thought I was done (Woo)", "vault_lines": ["You must have thought I was—, you must have thought I was done (Woo)"], "meaning": "In this line it sounds like Yeat is saying “new level”, however in the next line he’s clearly saying “You must’ve thought I was”.\n\nIt appears that Yeat was attempting to freestyle this bar in one take after saying “Cut off they head, cut off they tops, bitch” which results in the first time of him saying “You must have thought I was” being slurred in a way that sounds like “new level” or “don’t know who else” before catching what he was trying to say.\n\nTechnically this line cannot be transcribed as what he’s saying is entirely nonsensical, but it’s filled in due to it being clear what he was really trying to say.\n\nYeat has been known to use freestyling as a technique for more flexibility over flowing instead of directly punching in, a song where he notably does this is on “Mëan feen”.", "annotation_id": "34255312", "url": "https://genius.com/34255312", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "LYING 5 FUN", "line": "Bitch, I’m a rich-ass motherfucker,", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, I'm a rich-ass motherfucker,"], "meaning": "", "annotation_id": "33812096", "url": "https://genius.com/33812096", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": false, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "LYING 5 FUN", "line": "Hey, huh, cut off they head, cut off they tops, bitch (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["Hey, huh, cut off they head, cut off they tops, bitch (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Throughout the LYFESTYLE album, Yeat has expressed cutting off people heads/tops frequently.", "annotation_id": "33812092", "url": "https://genius.com/33812092", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "LYING 5 FUN", "line": "You must have thought I was done", "vault_lines": ["You must have thought I was done"], "meaning": "You see, for this line, the genius that is Yeat refers back to the song that this one’s named after, saying “you must have thought I was done” which is what he says midway through his song ‘lying 4 fun’.\n\nThis is a powerful line because the original song is actually roughly double the length of a normal song on the album, which means you would be inclined to think the song is beginning to end, before year interrupts the outro by saying “you must have thought I was done” and beginning an entirely ‘new’ song in the same song\n\nWhat a truly great lyrical masterpiece", "annotation_id": "33663493", "url": "https://genius.com/33663493", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "LYING 5 FUN", "line": "Yeah, I’m hooked on highs,", "vault_lines": ["Look here, a thot, yeah, I'm hooked on highs,"], "meaning": "“Yeah, i’m hooked on the highs”\n\nYeat is referencing his “geeker” journey through being high, making some attempts to quit, but ends up back to being on the drugs. Therefore its him being “hooked” on the highs.", "annotation_id": "33588946", "url": "https://genius.com/33588946", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "LYING 5 FUN", "line": "Like I told you they foreign again, they foreign checks, huh, huh We don’t do foreign checks, they bounce We don’t do foreign checks, they bounce", "vault_lines": ["Like I told you they foreign again, they foreign checks, huh, huh"], "meaning": "Yeat is talking about financial transactions and the unreliability of foreign checks. When he mentions “foreign checks,” he’s referring to checks from international banks that may not be trusted or easily processed. By saying that “they bounce,” he’s saying that these checks often fail to clear, so they are not reliable forms of payment.", "annotation_id": "33345356", "url": "https://genius.com/33345356", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "LYING 5 FUN", "line": "And we keep sittin’ outside, we can get you chopped, bitch, huh (Yeah, yeah)", "vault_lines": ["And we keep sittin' outside, we can get you chopped, bitch, huh (Yeah, yeah)"], "meaning": "Yeat is emphasizing a sense of danger and readiness to retaliate. The phrase “we can get you chopped” is a slang term meaning he can harm or eliminate his enemies, this portrays a tough stance and highlights the consequences of messing with yeat.", "annotation_id": "33345319", "url": "https://genius.com/33345319", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "LYING 5 FUN", "line": "All of my gang familia, all of my gang familiar, uh", "vault_lines": ["All of my gang familia, all of my gang familiar, uh"], "meaning": "All of his gang is Familia, meaning family and which may also be a reference to Yeats song on 2093 “Familia”. Here, Yeat is implying that his gang is Family and they are familiar to him.", "annotation_id": "33343084", "url": "https://genius.com/33343084", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
