{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Jus Wokëup", "line": "​", "vault_lines": ["Ha (Woo, woo, woo, woo)"], "meaning": "In this line, Yeat is saying he had two cups from Exotic Pop delivered to his AirBnb in San Francisco, using Uber Eats, from an Exotic Pop location named 711 North Wilson Way, Stockton, CA 95205, USA", "annotation_id": "34137744", "url": "https://genius.com/34137744", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Jus Wokëup", "line": "Sometimes I be so high, I can’t even see", "vault_lines": ["Sometimes I be so high, I can't even see"], "meaning": "Yeat rapped a quadruple entendre in just ONE LINE.\n\nYeat states that he is so high off of drugs, that his vision is messed up. But, Yeat also disses the music industry by saying he is so high in his career and vision of the music industry is so blurred, all the other artists are the same to him. “High” and “see” together sound like the fruit drink, “Hi-C”, which is a metaphor for Yeat’s youth and how he wishes sometimes not to be so high in status.\n\n “See” also sounds like the letter “C”, which is the third letter of the alphabet. If you take every third letter from the lyric, you get: “misehhaene” which is what someone who is fucking high out of their mind would say.\n\nAnd that’s how,", "annotation_id": "32599108", "url": "https://genius.com/32599108", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Jus Wokëup", "line": "Yeah, I was lazy as hell, I Uber Eat-ed two cups (Yeah, huh) San-Fran’, 711, soda from ExoticPop", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I was lazy as hell, I Uber Eat-ed two cups (Yeah, huh)"], "meaning": "This lyric could be misinterpreted as ‘dumb’, however, that is not the case. Yeat is actually providing a meta commentary on society’s over reliance on technology to facilitate instant gratification, and how it has led to an increasingly lazy population.\n\nHe even uses an improper verb ‘eat-ed’ to accentuate his point.\n\nLyrical genius.", "annotation_id": "32329357", "url": "https://genius.com/32329357", "state": "pending", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
