{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IM YEAT", "line": "I tried to stay down with the same people, but I can’t change what I can’t (I’m Yeat)", "vault_lines": ["I tried to stay down with the same people, but I can't change what I can't (I'm Yeat)"], "meaning": "In this line Yeat appears to be referring to his fallout with his friend and sidekick SeptembersRich.\n\n\n\nAccording to Yeat’s ex-girlfriend Symone Ryley, SeptembersRich was accused showing signs of jealousy towards Yeat, also milking his name and competing with him by trying to be sober.\n\nYeat is saying he can’t change SeptembersRich, and he will remain the same.", "annotation_id": "36831011", "url": "https://genius.com/36831011", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IM YEAT", "line": "Yeah, and you know I’m a geeker, baby, I’ma do it every day of the week (Geek), I’m Yeat", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, and you know I'm a geeker, baby, I'ma do it every day of the week (Geek), I'm Yeat"], "meaning": "Yeat is saying that he gets high every day of the week.", "annotation_id": "36796103", "url": "https://genius.com/36796103", "state": "pending", "votes": -4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IM YEAT", "line": "I just flew out a new bitch next day, she just came out from Chile", "vault_lines": ["I just flew out a new bitch next day, she just came out from Chile"], "meaning": "Next day international shipping is typically the fastest and most expensive international shipping option. Yeat is likening himself flying out a new romantic partner to next day shipping.", "annotation_id": "36663267", "url": "https://genius.com/36663267", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IM YEAT", "line": "How you doin’? Nice to meet you, but fuck you, I don’t need you, bitch (I’m, I’m), I’m Yeat", "vault_lines": ["How you doin'? Nice to meet you, but fuck you, I don't need you, bitch (I'm, I'm), I'm Yeat"], "meaning": "Some have speculated that this is a diss towards Lil Uzi Vert, but it is highly unlikely because recently Yeat has posted a music snippet with him and Uzi in it.", "annotation_id": "36517959", "url": "https://genius.com/36517959", "state": "pending", "votes": -7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IM YEAT", "line": "Get the chrome wheels spinnin’ again", "vault_lines": ["Get the chrome wheels spinnin' again"], "meaning": "“Chrome wheels” is a niche slang for Chrome Hearts Wheel Rims, which wealthy rappers are known for placing on their expensive cars which makes the appearance of the car even more flamboyant.\n\nThis is because Chrome Hearts is a known expensive, near exorbitant, brand for their application of chrome into accessories, ranging from simple rings, to really whatever you’d like from them.\n\n\n\n A picture showing a part of Drake's ultimate Chrome Hearts Rolls Royce Cullinan, with the Chrome Hearts rim (chrome wheels) in the middle of the frame\n\n\nAlso, notably, in the official music video during this line, Yeat is pointing and spinning his hand towards the rims of his Ferrari, and whilst it might be confusing as the rims to that car appear to be made out of generic, instead of Chrome Hearts metal, Yeat, being known for incorporating his “flexing” lifestyle in a lot of his music, is more likely refering towards Chrome Hearts Rims, instead of generic wheel rims made out of chrome.", "annotation_id": "36320401", "url": "https://genius.com/36320401", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IM YEAT", "line": "If you see the money, that’s Yeat If you smell the money, that’s Yeat If you feel the money, that’s Yeat", "vault_lines": ["If you see the money, that's Yeat"], "meaning": "Of the 5 senses Yeat refers to 3 of them. Sight (See the money) Smell (Smell the money) and finally touch (Feel the money). All roads lead back to Yeat.", "annotation_id": "36287379", "url": "https://genius.com/36287379", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IM YEAT", "line": "(Yeet-yah, yah-yeet-yah) (Yah-yeet-yah, yah-yeet-yah) (Yah-yeet-yah, yah-yeet-yah) (Yah-yeet-yah, yah-yeet-yah)", "vault_lines": ["What a day, what a show, man"], "meaning": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Sr02tSyhCWA\n\nThis sample comes from the meme that became popular on vine and may be a factor in yeat choosing his stage name\n\nYeat explaining how he chose his name –  “When people hear my name they feel like they already heard of me” – Yeat in an interview", "annotation_id": "36279180", "url": "https://genius.com/36279180", "state": "pending", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IM YEAT", "line": "I ain’t ever want the Rollie (Whoo, whoo) Bitch, this Rollie wanted me (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I ain't ever want the Rollie (Whoo, whoo)"], "meaning": "A possible reference to “CC (No More Rollies)”:\n\nYeah, no, no, I don’t want the rollie, it want me, yeah (It want me)", "annotation_id": "36244532", "url": "https://genius.com/36244532", "state": "pending", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IM YEAT", "line": "I went shark on this bitch, tell her, “Yam”", "vault_lines": ["I went shark on this bitch, tell her, \"Yam\""], "meaning": "Yeat is referencing his song “Yam” which was released by the producer Sharkboy, and features a shark’s dorsal fin on the cover art of the song.", "annotation_id": "36202172", "url": "https://genius.com/36202172", "state": "pending", "votes": 39, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IM YEAT", "status": "done", "targets": 25, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Yeat-and-bnyx-im-yeat-lyrics", "genius_title": "IM YEAT", "genius_id": 12106022, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "ILUV", "line": "I seen 2093 and fuckin’ came back, yeah, every time, yeah I’m tryna tell you something, but you won’t listen to your mind And it’s fine, it don’t surprise me but I-", "vault_lines": ["I seen 2093 and fuckin' came back, yeah, every time, yeah"], "meaning": "Throughout his career, Yeat has portrayed himself as an artist living beyond the present, imagining alien contact, advanced tech, and transcendence, while also suggesting he is intellectually above ordinary humans.\n\nIn his Channel 5 interview, he spoke about wanting to go six million years into the future to experience alien life and futuristic culture, an idea that has become one of the central themes of his artistic identity. Similarly, in a COMPLEX interview, he described himself as existing “in his own world,” constantly moving beyond trends and his own past creations.\n\nYeat expanded on these ideas in a 2024 032c magazine, describing himself as a futurist who communicates better with numbers than words, drawing a parallel to the way aliens communicate.\n\nThis statement reinforces Yeat’s alien-like persona, revealing his struggle to communicate and connect with others, which also appears in his 2021 track “Wit my alien”:\n\nSlur my words, I barely could even talk to ‘em\n\nYeat often talks about the future and mentions aliens. In the same 032c magazine, he claimed he met aliens as a child, saying it felt completely real and made him question whether he’s truly from Earth, the same thing he mentioned in his Instagram story:\n\n\n\nIn the NardwuarServiette interview, Yeat was gifted a vintage E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial vinyl record and read-along book. He then mentioned that he likes the movie, which is about an alien living among humans who struggles to communicate and be understood, something that also reflects him.", "annotation_id": "37320098", "url": "https://genius.com/37320098", "state": "accepted", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "ILUV", "line": "But I know you tryin’ not listen to me (I love, I love), yeah, but I know you love it", "vault_lines": ["But I know you tryin’ not listen to me (I love, I love), yeah, but I know you love it"], "meaning": "Ever since Yeat’s initial rise to prominence in the rap scene, he’s had more than his fair share of haters. It’s not uncommon for many listeners to hate Yeat’s music upon their first introduction, only to see themselves become a fan once they’ve become accustomed to his sound. Whether it be music, politics, movies, etc. a lot of people don’t like to change their opinions on things when they’ve already taken a different position, which can lead people to force themselves to act like they still hold their original stance even when they secretly don’t. Popular opinion often influences people’s personal opinions as well, or at least their expression of them. If everyone else around you is hating on a music artist you like, you might be inclined to hide the fact that you enjoy their material.", "annotation_id": "30913965", "url": "https://genius.com/30913965", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "ILUV", "line": "I love, I love", "vault_lines": ["But I know you tryin’ not listen to me (I love, I love), yeah, but I know you love it"], "meaning": "This vocal snippet, as well as the haunting synths in the background, are sampled from the Crystal Castles song “Fleece”.\nCrystal Castles makes pretty futuristic sounding music, and may have been an inspiration for Yeat when creating this album.", "annotation_id": "30913934", "url": "https://genius.com/30913934", "state": "pending", "votes": 51, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "ILUV", "line": "How long can you stand with me? (I love) How long can you stand for me?", "vault_lines": ["I love it, I love it"], "meaning": "Yeat here tests the loyalty of those who love him by asking them if they’ll stay with him forever, or if their love for him is only temporary.", "annotation_id": "30912476", "url": "https://genius.com/30912476", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "ILUV", "line": "I’ma buy your fuckin’ company and sell your hard work for a dime, uh", "vault_lines": ["I'ma buy your fuckin' company and sell your hard work for a dime, uh"], "meaning": "Here, Yeat implies being so rich and powerful that he could reduce everything his enemies produce to nothing – any hard work that people he dislikes do is going to mean nothing because he can just buy it and sell it for nothing.", "annotation_id": "30912471", "url": "https://genius.com/30912471", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "ILUV", "line": "I heard you tryna die (I love, I love), so here’s a-, uh, huh, here’s a boulder", "vault_lines": ["I heard you tryna die (I love, I love), so here's a-, uh, huh, here's a boulder"], "meaning": "This is a reference to the tale of Sisyphus.The tale involves Sisyphus being punished in the underworld with a boulder and being forced to roll it up a hill, but when the boulder reaches the top it rolls back down.\n\nYeat is continuing the theme that he is God by saying he can punish people by forcing them to relive the experience of Sisyphus for eternity.", "annotation_id": "30904295", "url": "https://genius.com/30904295", "state": "pending", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "ILUV", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Yeat-iluv-lyrics", "genius_title": "ILUV", "genius_id": 10060352, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Punt", "vault_lines": ["We on the side and we punt"], "meaning": "kicking a football (american football)", "annotation_id": "36910516", "url": "https://genius.com/36910516", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "I put a check up on my neck", "vault_lines": ["I put a check up on my neck"], "meaning": "check refers to alot of money and yeat puts diamond chains on his neck", "annotation_id": "36910499", "url": "https://genius.com/36910499", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "My dawg in the can like a metal lid Three the kid, three the kid, three the kid", "vault_lines": ["My dawg in the can like a metal lid"], "meaning": "“In the can” is slang for being in jail. Drake plays on the homonym “can” by metaphorically comparing his friend’s incarceration to a metal lid inside a literal can.\n\n“Free the kid” is a plea for his boy’s release, but Drizzy flips it further by playing on the homophones “free” and “three”, repeating free three times.", "annotation_id": "36283852", "url": "https://genius.com/36283852", "state": "accepted", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "(BNYX)", "vault_lines": ["I called up my twizz' (BNYX)"], "meaning": "Benjamin Saint Fort (born April 23, 1995), professionally known as BNYX®, (pronounced Benny X), is a record producer from Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. He is known for his production for Drake, Yeat, Lil Uzi Vert, Travis Scott, and Lancey Foux, among others.\n\nBNYX® acquired his pseudonym as a result of taking his nickname, Benny, and attaching it to the letter X, which he describes stands for “extreme,” a quality that he strives to achieve when creating music.\n\nIn 2021, BNYX® signed to record label and production group, Working on Dying.\n\nI-I-I’m working on dying, BNYX", "annotation_id": "31027083", "url": "https://genius.com/31027083", "state": "pending", "votes": 18, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "All of my twizz’ got a gun (Blam) Couple of my twizz’ on the run (Phew)", "vault_lines": ["All of my twizz' got a gun (Blam)"], "meaning": "In this line; Yeat is referring to his “twizzies” (which are his homies), by saying how they all posses firearms and how they are on the run for possibly doing something wrong.", "annotation_id": "31027010", "url": "https://genius.com/31027010", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Luh geek", "vault_lines": ["And I'm poppin' these Percs just for fun (Luh geek"], "meaning": "Luh Geeky is a Yeatanese rapper who was featured on Yeat’s song ‘Now’\nYou can hear him moaning very loudly in the background saying “I got bands now.”\n\nLuh Geek would translate into English as little high which Yeat would be after ‘poppin’ these percs' in the line this is the adlib to.\n\nYeat also has an unreleased song called “luh geeky” https://on.soundcloud.com/p4sYVnbBQp4M2cDz6", "annotation_id": "29875976", "url": "https://genius.com/29875976", "state": "pending", "votes": 30, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "If you did the crime, bitch, why you cryin’?", "vault_lines": ["If you did the crime, bitch, why you cryin'?"], "meaning": "Taking to Kantian ethics, Yeat utilizes a detonological questioning of his lover, whether a female domesticated dog or a partner referred to as such, on why they are catalyzing moisture from their tear ducts over inconsequential affairs that apparently have broken the purview of the law", "annotation_id": "29870104", "url": "https://genius.com/29870104", "state": "pending", "votes": 25, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "I signed for a couple milli’, you only touched a hundred thou’", "vault_lines": ["I signed for a couple milli', you only touched a hundred thou'"], "meaning": "“I signed for a couple milli'” means that Yeat has signed a record deal or contract with a music label, and as a result, he’s set to make several million dollars from this deal. This is a common theme in rap music where artists often sign lucrative contracts with major record labels.\n\nThe second part of the line, “you only touched a hundred thou',” is a way of saying that the person he’s addressing has only made or earned $100,000. In the context of the lyrics, Yeat is emphasizing the vast difference in their financial achievements. He’s saying that while he’s set to make millions through his music career, the other person has only managed to earn $100,000, which is significantly less. This is a common theme in hip-hop lyrics, where artists frequently boast about their wealth and success, often in a competitive or confrontational manner.\nAdditionally, Yeat references a lyric from a previous collaborator of his, Autumn!. Since 2021, the two had not been on good terms, and previously, Autumn! stated in one of the duo’s songs, Probably!:\n\nAin’t got a million, it’s more like a hundred thousand in my bank\".", "annotation_id": "29841095", "url": "https://genius.com/29841095", "state": "pending", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "I stay with that O like a tire I stay with that O like a bagel", "vault_lines": ["I stay with that O like a tire"], "meaning": "O here could mean multiple things, such as a zero in an amount of money. But the more likely references are to an ounce of illicit substances, or the other members of Drake’s OVO label that are referenced in the previous line.\n\nObviously a tied knot and a bagel are both in the shape of circles, or O’s.", "annotation_id": "29814060", "url": "https://genius.com/29814060", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "I stay with that O like a lifesaver", "vault_lines": ["I stay with that O like a lifesaver"], "meaning": "Lifesavers are an O shaped mint/candy, intentionally such that when choked on there is still a hole to breath through (that’s why their name is Lifesavers)", "annotation_id": "29773588", "url": "https://genius.com/29773588", "state": "accepted", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "My Glocky, it sit on the night table You light on the waist like a lightsaber", "vault_lines": ["My Glocky, it sit on the night table"], "meaning": "Referring to his “glock,” Drake keeps his piece on his night table, juxtaposing his opp, who is “light on the waist” a double entendre meaning they are absent of a weapon, also a lightsaber being a “light” weapon holstered on the hip", "annotation_id": "29733109", "url": "https://genius.com/29733109", "state": "pending", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Comin’ live from the one after five Comin’ live from the three, two time", "vault_lines": ["Look, comin' live from the"], "meaning": "One after five and two times three are both equal six. Drake says he is comin' live from the six, which is another name for Toronto, Drake’s hometown.\nThe reason Toronto is called the six is because of the six municipalities making up Metro Toronto before they were all joined in 1998. It is also derived from the main area code in Toronto, 416.", "annotation_id": "29726392", "url": "https://genius.com/29726392", "state": "accepted", "votes": 33, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "In Seattle, it’s just me and Twizz’", "vault_lines": ["In Seattle, it's just me and Twizz'"], "meaning": "Drake calls back to when he stopped in Seattle during his “It’s All A Blur” Tour and brought him out for a shoutout.\n\nhttps://x.com/Kurrco/status/1695416005852279280?s=20", "annotation_id": "29725051", "url": "https://genius.com/29725051", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "October’s Own, it’s a label", "vault_lines": ["October's Own, it's a label"], "meaning": "October’s Very Own, or OVO (officially known as OVO Sound), is Drake’s music label. OVO was founded by Drake, 40, and Oliver El-Khatib.\n\nThe phrase “October’s Own is a Label” is a reference to the line “YSL is a label”, which was widely used on social media during the RICO case against YSL, Young Thug, and Gunna.\nPolice used lyrics from Young Thug’s music to connect him to gang related crimes. Drake frequently alludes to his mob ties (even got a song named “Mob Ties”) and connection to the streets. This line is telling any feds watching his movements that OVO is a record label and not a criminal organization.", "annotation_id": "29725036", "url": "https://genius.com/29725036", "state": "accepted", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "That ain’t gon’ fly, nope", "vault_lines": ["That ain't gon' fly, nope"], "meaning": "Drake mimics Yeat’s “low voice” that can be seen on some parts of this song.", "annotation_id": "29724957", "url": "https://genius.com/29724957", "state": "accepted", "votes": 40, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Whole gang fuckin’ eastbound and they down just like Danny McBride", "vault_lines": ["Whole gang fuckin' eastbound and they down just like Danny McBride"], "meaning": "Danny McBride is the leading actor, co-creator and co-writer of the sports comedy television series “Eastbound & Down”.", "annotation_id": "29724869", "url": "https://genius.com/29724869", "state": "accepted", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Traveling forever in the dark Darkness into blackness There and back, it’s always black Oh-ah, it screeched across the sky Nothing to do but lie", "vault_lines": ["Traveling forever in the dark"], "meaning": "This track samples Azimuth’s 1977 song “The Tunnel”:\n\nhttps://youtu.be/LRFqc1lUxqQ?si=X0jIObk1dQjtZr7g&t=158", "annotation_id": "29724830", "url": "https://genius.com/29724830", "state": "accepted", "votes": 151, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "I just got off the phone with my demon and he told me that I’m gon’ win", "vault_lines": ["I just got off the phone with my demon and he told me that I'm gon' win"], "meaning": "Yeat has mentioned many times that he talks with his demons, and that they have helped him succeed in the industry.\n\nIn his track, “Monëy so big”, Yeat raps:\n\nI got the devil on my phone, he told me, keep doin' this shit\nYeah, devil taught me how to get rich", "annotation_id": "29625877", "url": "https://genius.com/29625877", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Big body Lamb’ truck", "vault_lines": ["Big body Lamb' truck"], "meaning": "Yeat has said in previous songs that he rides in his Lamb' truck, a Lamborghini Urus. He sometimes calls his Urus (along with his Mercedes-Benz GLE) a “Tonka”. This term can be appointed to any of his big body vehicles.\n\nIn “Tonka 2”, Yeat raps about it, saying\n\nTonka, this a big truck, this a Lamb' truck\n\n\n\nHe commonly refers to his Tonka in many tracks as “Big body”, he says this because It’s a Urus. Unlike traditional Lamborghini’s, it is an SUV, with a big body. More examples where he refers to it being:\n\n\nRollin\nBig tonka\nDouble", "annotation_id": "29560142", "url": "https://genius.com/29560142", "state": "pending", "votes": 29, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "IDGAF", "line": "Couple of my twizz’ sellin’ Runtz (Luh geek)", "vault_lines": ["Couple of my twizz' sellin' Runtz (Luh geek)"], "meaning": "Some of Yeat’s friends (of which he refers to as twizzies) get income from selling Runtz, a stronger strand of Marijuana.\n\nYeat has previously sold drugs like weed and cocaine, and has made these friends from affiliation.\n\nWhile Yeat has moved on from selling drugs and has become a rapper, they haven’t.", "annotation_id": "29560078", "url": "https://genius.com/29560078", "state": "pending", "votes": 36, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
