{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Hëy", "line": "I went and tatted a demon on me then this shit started to happen, this shit start to work", "vault_lines": ["I went and tatted a demon on me then this shit started to happen, this shit start to work"], "meaning": "Yeat makes constant references to the idea of making deals with the devil/demons for money and fame. In “Monëy So Big” yeat says “𝘐 𝘨𝘰𝘵 𝘵𝘩𝘦 𝘥𝘦𝘷𝘪𝘭 𝘰𝘯 𝘮𝘺 𝘱𝘩𝘰𝘯𝘦, 𝘩𝘦 𝘵𝘰𝘭𝘥 𝘮𝘦 𝘬𝘦𝘦𝘱 𝘥𝘰𝘪𝘯 𝘵𝘩𝘪𝘴 𝘴𝘩𝘪𝘵”. While it is unclear what Yeat explicitly meant here, but on Lying 4 Fun, this brief verse definitely seems to elude to a deal of some sort. “𝘓𝘪𝘯𝘬𝘦𝘥 𝘸𝘪𝘵𝘩 𝘵𝘩𝘦 𝘥𝘦𝘷𝘪𝘭, 𝘵𝘰𝘭𝘥 𝘩𝘪𝘮 𝘐 𝘫𝘶𝘴𝘵 𝘯𝘦𝘦𝘥 𝘢 𝘴𝘪𝘨𝘯𝘢𝘵𝘶𝘳𝘦, 𝘯𝘦𝘦𝘥 𝘩𝘪𝘴 𝘢𝘶𝘵𝘰𝘨𝘳𝘢𝘱𝘩\n𝘐 𝘢𝘪𝘯'𝘵 𝘯𝘦𝘷𝘦𝘳 𝘴𝘰𝘭𝘥 𝘮𝘺 𝘴𝘰𝘶𝘭 𝘣𝘶𝘵 𝘐 𝘫𝘶𝘴𝘵 𝘥𝘰𝘯𝘢𝘵𝘦𝘥 𝘪𝘵 𝘵𝘰 𝘺𝘰𝘶\n𝘐 𝘫𝘶𝘴𝘵 𝘵𝘢𝘵𝘵𝘦𝘥 𝘢𝘭𝘭 𝘵𝘩𝘢𝘵 𝘴𝘩𝘪𝘵 𝘰𝘯 𝘮𝘦, 𝘵𝘩𝘦𝘯 𝘪𝘵 𝘩𝘢𝘱𝘱𝘦𝘯𝘦𝘥, 𝘣𝘪𝘵𝘤𝘩, 𝘪𝘵'𝘴 𝘵𝘰𝘰 𝘵𝘳𝘶𝘦 (𝘸𝘩𝘢𝘵?)\n𝘉𝘪𝘵𝘤𝘩, 𝘐 𝘮𝘢𝘥𝘦 𝘢 𝘤𝘰𝘶𝘱𝘭𝘦 𝘔'𝘴, 𝘯𝘰𝘵 𝘰𝘧𝘧 𝘳𝘢𝘱𝘱𝘪𝘯𝘨, 𝘺𝘦𝘢𝘩, 𝘣𝘶𝘵 𝘸𝘩𝘰 𝘬𝘯𝘦𝘸?\"", "annotation_id": "25401311", "url": "https://genius.com/25401311", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Hëy", "line": "Hop in that Cayenne, you know that it swerve", "vault_lines": ["Hop in that Cayenne, you know that it swerve"], "meaning": "Yeat explains in this line two things. For starters, that he owns a Porsche Cayenne, an expensive SUV that drives very similarly to a sports car. Yeat has referenced this specific car on other songs such as Told ya. Secondly, Yeat states that he drives extremely quickly and/or recklessly, thus illustrating his attitude of carelessness.\n\n\n\n A picture of a 2020 Porsche Cayenne", "annotation_id": "25065075", "url": "https://genius.com/25065075", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Hëy", "line": "I pull up, I pull up to Follies, I throw my money then I’m watching it twerk", "vault_lines": ["I pull up, I pull up to Follies, I throw my money then I'm watching it twerk"], "meaning": "Yeat is stating that he arrives (“pull up to”) at Follies, a popular strip club in Atlanta. The next segment of this line describes how Yeat throws his money (presumably at Follies). Finally, Yeat ends the line with a double entendre: “then I’m watching it twerk”. “It” could refer to two things, a woman’s ass or Yeat’s bills that he has thrown on the dancers of this club. Yeat has mentioned multiple times that his money twerks (see Money Twërk), so this is not unlikely. However, “it” could also refer to the strippers at Follies, and he is watching them twerk.\n\nUnfortunately, Follies is closed permanently due to legal reasons.", "annotation_id": "25065055", "url": "https://genius.com/25065055", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
