{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "HARD 2 MISS", "line": "Whole lot of chains, Elliante, boy, I pissed ’em (Pissed ’em) Whole lot of diamonds on that boy, it’s hard to miss ’em (Shine)", "vault_lines": ["Whole lot of chains, Elliante, boy, I pissed 'em (Pissed 'em)"], "meaning": "Elliot Elliante is a New York jeweler that makes chains and other jewelry for rappers like Yeat and many many others\n\nWith a lot of diamonds, his neck shines bright so its hard to miss someone who stands out like that.", "annotation_id": "39236111", "url": "https://genius.com/39236111", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "HARD 2 MISS", "line": "They done seen the kid blow up like I’m Osama son (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["They done seen the kid blow up like I'm Osama son (Yeah)"], "meaning": "This line is a double entendre:\n1. Refers to Osama Bin Laden (first general emir of al-Qaedal), a terrorist known for explosive attacks. This is where the “blow up” part comes from. Of course, in Yeat’s case, this references his explosion in popularity.\n2. Refers to rapper and Slayworld member Osamason, someone Yeat has referenced in other songs, and compares his rise in fame.", "annotation_id": "36126163", "url": "https://genius.com/36126163", "state": "pending", "votes": -7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "HARD 2 MISS", "line": "All I need is more cash All I need is more whips All I need is more bags All I need is way more bitches to eat me up in this Maybach", "vault_lines": ["All I need is more cash"], "meaning": "In these lines, coming directly after his admissions of suicidal ideation and drug addiction, there is a deeper meaning to be found. While they may initially sound similar to hollow flexes or surface level desires, these types of statements serve as a vital coping mechanism for Yeat.\n\nInstead of focusing his energy and mind on resolving or healing his underlying problems previously confessed, he directs his mind to materialism and status. This serves as a distraction from what feels like an endless cycle of torment (as he has described).\n\nThis moment also features a contradiction of a previous lyric in the song. In a past line, Yeat mentions his worry of more money leading to more percs. However, in these lines, he blatantly states his desire for more money. This is a type of shift in personality that suggests internal instability. Here he is positively embracing the same thing (money) he previously stated may lead to his end.", "annotation_id": "36125143", "url": "https://genius.com/36125143", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "HARD 2 MISS", "line": "Yeah, I’ve been at the edge I’ve been at the edge (Whip it) I’ve been at the edge", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I've been at the edge"], "meaning": "Yeat’s repetition of feeling or being “at the edge” refers to  his mental state and emotional feeling at the time. Being on the edge means being close enough to jump (close enough to make the decision) but still on the ground, even if ever so slightly. This means Yeat feels he may tip or fall off into the abyss at any point, but deep down, he knows there may still be hope.", "annotation_id": "36125108", "url": "https://genius.com/36125108", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "HARD 2 MISS", "line": "But I worry, when I go up, won’t it get worse? (Money) More money coming in mean more Percs (What?)", "vault_lines": ["But I worry, when I go up, won't it get worse? (Money)"], "meaning": "Expressing his worry and concern, Yeat discusses the future. He states that if he is to gain more money (which he almost certainly knows will happen), his issues will likely intensify rather than ease. He admits completely to having no control over himself and his actions regarding drugs. He believes that more money = more drugs (specifically percocets: a powerful opioid).\n\nWhile many yearn for fame and wealth, Yeat showcases the negative sides of them. More money and popularity means easier access to drugs and negative temptations. He has made it clear in his music (at least in his experience) that money does not solve internal problems.\n\nThere is an important juxtaposition between “going up” and “getting worse” in these lines. This contrast emphasizes and encapsulates the tragic paradox of chasing success while feeling perpetually trapped by it.", "annotation_id": "36125094", "url": "https://genius.com/36125094", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "HARD 2 MISS", "line": "The world seen too many goated rappers drop dead, that’s fed I can’t be another one of them so I’ma go up instead (High)", "vault_lines": ["The world seen too many goated rappers drop dead, that's fed"], "meaning": "Taking a wide scope and cultural observation, Yeat knows the world has seen too many premature deaths (especially from rappers considering the common glorification of abusing hard drugs) and he refuses (or tries his hardest) to now become one of them.\n\nHis reference to the “fed” is likely a general reference, possibly referring to both the literal feds (police forces, FBI, DEA, etc) and the symbolic meaning (the actual system and machine controlling all). Yeat knows many of these systems are engineered, or at the very least, enabled by a structure that profits off of the artists, while slowly (or occasionally quickly) destroying them.\n\nThis interpretation is supported by the anti-police and anti-authority lines present throughout Yeat’s discography such as in “Overloaded” from 2021:\n“You’re federal, you sounding like the cops, yeah pissable.”\n\nYeat feels the only option other than going down (death or failure) is going up. However, as will be shown in the following lines, he fears this rise may cause his inevitable demise.", "annotation_id": "36124976", "url": "https://genius.com/36124976", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "HARD 2 MISS", "line": "I off the truce, stuck now with the times I thought ’bout killing (Yeah, yeah) Myself and somebody else ’cause I got problems", "vault_lines": ["I off the truce, stuck now with the times I thought 'bout killing (Yeah, yeah)"], "meaning": "Beginning by stating that he is “off the truce” signals the end to a period of peace (and the implication of future chaos). His “truce” was likely with himself, and now that it is over, he feels stuck with his negative thoughts.\n\nAlthough, “negative thoughts” is likely a major understatement, considering his admission to suicidal and homicidal ideation. The word “stuck” suggests that the difficult thoughts and desires Yeat deals with are not fleeting nor temporary, but instead, persistent and inescapable.\n\nHis explanation: “cause I got problems” is purposefully vague and feels almost like an offhand justification to his behavior. The contrast between the understatement and indeterminateness of “problems” following the gravity of the previous lines highlights a numbness and possible neglect to understand his own situation (or to seek help).", "annotation_id": "36121185", "url": "https://genius.com/36121185", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "HARD 2 MISS", "line": "I can’t get realer than the shit I’m telling you now, bitch, I’m spillin’ (Ooh)", "vault_lines": ["I can't get realer than the shit I'm telling you now, bitch, I'm spillin' (Ooh)"], "meaning": "Yeat tells the listener that he cannot possibly be any more real or honest with them, and that he is currently “spillin” his thoughts openly. This line, while likely referring to the entire song, seems to be particularly associated with the following lines (especially those in the chorus and post-chorus) and acts as a kind of frame for the confessions: suicidal ideation, drug abuse, and the endless cycle of disappointment.\n\nThe placement of “bitch” in this line is far from unusual for Yeat. While its usage reinforces his standard hypermasculine delivery, it does not negate the vulnerability being shown. In fact, the inclusion of this word (used entirely for filler) may very well reflect a desire to appear harder and more aggressive than he truly feels. Maintaining this front, even when in the midst of what seems to be an emotional breakdown, appears to be one of Yeat’s main coping mechanisms.\n\nEven when Yeat is being honest, he is unable to separate himself from the ingrained coping mechanisms and identity structures that the persona he has built depends on.", "annotation_id": "36120933", "url": "https://genius.com/36120933", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "HARD 2 MISS", "line": "I said I’d quit the Percs, it’s been one day, I already miss ’em", "vault_lines": ["I said I'd quit the Percs, it's been one day, I already miss 'em"], "meaning": "Yeat states that he attempted to quit percocets but already misses them after only a singular day. This is likely to imply that he will relapse soon, or already has, and that this attempt to quit will have a similar outcome as his others.\n\nYeat has tried to quit drugs, or specific drugs, many times in the past (including before this song was recorded). This line carries with it a tragic weight, and suggests that even when Yeat attempts to change his bad habits, he feels inevitably unable.\n\nThis interpretation is reemphasized later in the song when Yeat states:\n“But I worry, when I go up, won’t it get worse?\nMore money coming in mean more Percs.”", "annotation_id": "36120691", "url": "https://genius.com/36120691", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "HARD 2 MISS", "line": "I be so high in the sky, yeah, I’m a missile (Ooh)", "vault_lines": ["I be so high in the sky, yeah, I'm a missile (Ooh)"], "meaning": "The surface meaning of this line is obvious: Yeat is high off drugs and comparing himself to a powerful, fast, and destructive missile flying through the sky. This is likely how he feels in the moment.\n\nQuickly, however, the troubling deeper meaning, especially when examining the surrounding lines, becomes clear. Yeat is comparing himself to a missile not just due to its speed or power, but because of what a missile truly is and what it represents: a powerful machine that is unable to land and only destroys. Missiles are not built for a safe return, and are instead designed to fly quickly with purpose until reaching its target destination, in which case, the machine explodes.\n\nBy calling himself a missile, Yeat seems to be expressing fear and worry that his current path (largely driven by drugs, emotional instability, fame, and meaningless sex) will end in disaster. The specific choice to make the comparison to a “missile” over any other flying object seems to place emphasis on the functionality of the missile. Missiles are typically unable to slow down (Yeat has never had intention to slow down) and unable to land (again, Yeat feels as though the ending to his story may be destructive). This general interpretation is supported by the following lines: especially his mention of suicide, desiring to kill others, and the idea that he cannot stop taking drugs.", "annotation_id": "36120607", "url": "https://genius.com/36120607", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "HARD 2 MISS", "line": "They mamas", "vault_lines": ["When I'm high you can hear my soul, you can hear it come out they mamas"], "meaning": "these mumbles", "annotation_id": "31670559", "url": "https://genius.com/31670559", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
