{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Goin’ Crazy", "line": "Walked up in Chanelly and dropped a twenty then I hit Margiela (Chane-ne)", "vault_lines": ["Walked up in Chanelly and dropped a twenty then I hit Margiela (Chane-ne)"], "meaning": "“Chane-ne” refers to the French fashion brand Chanel while “twenty” refers to $20,000. Yeat is flexing the fact that he can carelessly spend money at any time.\nHe additionally ends the line by mentioning another high-end fashion brand: Margiela.", "annotation_id": "35750198", "url": "https://genius.com/35750198", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Goin’ Crazy", "line": "Yeah, I talk to the devil weekly, I ain’t going to heaven (Eer, eer)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I talk to the devil weekly, I ain't going to heaven (Eer, eer)"], "meaning": "A devil reference, commonplace in Yeat’s songs, this time more direct than usual. He tells us that he will not be going to heaven, due to his acts, such as his discussions with the devil himself.\n\nPossibly referenced on Yeat & SeptembersRich’s collaboration “Met Tha Devil” released only 11 days later.\n“Going to Hell, going to Hell\nIf I had to choose, I would choose Hell”", "annotation_id": "35750163", "url": "https://genius.com/35750163", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Goin’ Crazy", "line": "Yeah, I pulled up the concert and got paid like it ain’t mine (Going crazy)", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I pulled up the concert and got paid like it ain't mine (Going crazy)"], "meaning": "In this lyric, it is suggesting that Yeat performed at a concert, and got payed very poorly. This is why he states “I pulled up to the concert and got payed like it ain’t mine”", "annotation_id": "33222772", "url": "https://genius.com/33222772", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Goin’ Crazy", "line": "These bitches snorting white, they snorting Ellen (Yeah) They listen to what I tell ‘em like a motherfuckin’ reverend (Ya, ya)", "vault_lines": ["These bitches snorting white, they snorting Ellen (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Yeat saying “These bitches snorting white, they snorting Ellen”, is a reference to Ellen the talk show host and cocaine which has a powdery white color, the same as her skin tone.\n\nReverends are preachers which speak to a crowd in a church setting, Yeat is saying that people listen to what he says through his music and he is speaking to them like a Reverend speaks to his church.", "annotation_id": "29338450", "url": "https://genius.com/29338450", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Goin’ Crazy", "line": "My Rollie got so bright, it’s hard for me to tell the time (Ack)", "vault_lines": ["My Rollie got so bright, it's hard for me to tell the time (Ack)"], "meaning": "A Rollie is a slang term used to refer to a Rolex watch, yeat says his Rolex watch is so shiny from the diamonds he can’t see the time.", "annotation_id": "29316940", "url": "https://genius.com/29316940", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Goin’ Crazy", "line": "All my diamonds flawless, all yours fake, I call you Cinderella", "vault_lines": ["All my diamonds flawless, all yours fake, I call you Cinderella"], "meaning": "Necessarily perhaps, with this bar Noah Oliver Smith is saying that all of the diamonds in his jewelry are flawless, which is the highest rating level a diamond can receive from GIA. GIA is the most highly recognized diamond certification grading service. Yeat compares his flawless diamonds to his haters diamonds which he claims to be phony, similar to how Cinderella’s slipper was glass instead of diamonds.\n\nPROPOSED SUGGESTION; The connection between the use of the word “fake” and “Cinderella” is revealed in the Cinderella movie, where in the beginning, Cinderella is an ugly bitch. However, once she comes to into contact with a fairy godmother with magical powers, she is transformed into a bad bitch. Due to the fact that she is only a bad bitch due to a spell, one might consider her beauty to be a facade, or in other words, fake.", "annotation_id": "28757308", "url": "https://genius.com/28757308", "state": "pending", "votes": 6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
