{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "GOD TALKIN SHHH", "line": "Real shit, that ***** ******* boy dead", "vault_lines": ["Real shit, that ***** ******* boy dead"], "meaning": "Earlier in the song, Yeat expresses confidence that he won’t face cancellation, regardless of his actions or words. In this line, he seems to start a word with “fa-” before censoring it, likely resembling “faggot” due to its length and sound. This could be a term he felt compelled to censor to avoid backlash or follow industry norms.\nAdditionally, the censoring could reference a former friend, like SeptembersRich or Nextel, as the following lines mention cutting off people who didn’t have his back and only real ones knowing the truth.\nAlternatively, there has been a different version of this song that has surfaced, where Yeat seems to be saying “Real shit, that phone manager boy dead”, but this is very unlikely to be the actual censored phrase, because the word “the” from the lyrics “the boy dead” after the censoring would be missing, indicating this is likely a different recording of this line, or potentially a stylistic edit to make it sound like he said something different.", "annotation_id": "33324236", "url": "https://genius.com/33324236", "state": "accepted", "votes": 28, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "GOD TALKIN SHHH", "line": "I was young when I seen them aliens come (Doo-doo-doo) They told me what to do and what to run (Eh)", "vault_lines": ["I was young when I seen them aliens come (Doo-doo-doo)"], "meaning": "Yeat refers to his alleged alien encounter at the age of eleven. This is shown in an interview, where he said:\n\nThe aliens, I mean, I will say one thing though, they are really tall—and looked almost human.", "annotation_id": "33319468", "url": "https://genius.com/33319468", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "GOD TALKIN SHHH", "line": "You say you get this bread—, but you work at Quiznos", "vault_lines": ["You say you get this bread—, but you work at Quiznos"], "meaning": "In this line, Yeat plays on the phrase “get this bread,” an expression that means to earn money, or succeed. By juxtaposing it with working at Quiznos, a fast-food sandwich chain, he is undercutting the image of prosperity that the speaker is trying to put forward. Bread is a primary ingredient of sandwiches, so Yeat shows that the speaker is literally using bread but isn’t getting the bread in a figurative sense.", "annotation_id": "33319414", "url": "https://genius.com/33319414", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "GOD TALKIN SHHH", "line": "Yeah, all them rats, all them mice, Swiss cheesy", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, all them rats, all them mice, Swiss cheesy"], "meaning": "“Rats” and “mice” are common slang for people who snitch or deceive. Swiss cheese, which is commonly associated with mice and rats, serves as a metaphor for exposing the rats' flaws or making holes in their credibility, much like the holes in Swiss cheese.", "annotation_id": "33319392", "url": "https://genius.com/33319392", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
