{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Dub", "line": "Seen you walking down the street, yeah, before you crossed", "vault_lines": ["Seen you walking down the street, yeah, before you crossed"], "meaning": "This bar is absolutely the most exquisite lyrical piece adapted into our new generation’s time of music but if you aren’t convinced by merely hearing the outstanding vocals that are Yeat’s, then allow me to explain why.\n\nYou see, this bar can be taken in multiple different intellectually perplexing ways that may be troubling for someone without as much brain power as someone like Yeat to understand. I am in the process of becoming one with my true purpose in life which is to decipher Yeat’s sayings down to the simplest of forms which is why I am able to just barely decode to you what such an amazing speaker has to say so without further ado, the explanation.\n\nThe first direction this bar could lead your mind is in the fact that Yeat’s opps don’t have much time to live when he catches them in person, in this example, walking down the street. This is because the fact that Yeat is on top of his game and deals with said opposition the moment he spots them, making them “cross” to the other side, in literal terms, they die.\n\nPhoto of someone crossing to the other side, presumably towards Heaven\n\n\nThe second extraordinary way this absolute mastermind of a lyric could be interpreted, which also happens to be my favorite way, is in the literal sense that Yeat’s opposition in fact crossed the street in order to avoid his presence.\n\n\n\nTo the simpleton's eye, it seems like a blind man crossing the street in this photo, however, that is no blind man, in fact, this man specifically chose this attire in the slim chance that he ran into his enemy, Yeat.  \nThis is an example of the outrageous measures oppositions of Noah Oliver Smith go to in order to save themselves from his clutches.\n\n\nYou see, a common street usually has two sidewalks, the side Yeat is walking on and the side he isn’t on. In Yeat’s position, he doesn’t mind which side he is on because he isn’t worried about anyone or anything getting in his way.\nOn the contrary however, Yeat’s said opposition is nowhere near in the same mindset as Mr. Yeat in the sense that he is absolutely terrified when he see’s Noah Oliver Smith walking down the same path as him, causing him to cross the street in fear.\n\nUnfortunately however this bar explains to the audience that Yeat already spotted the depicted enemy which most-likely means he already handled the unlucky suspect and they are no longer with us. They have “crossed”.", "annotation_id": "27170677", "url": "https://genius.com/27170677", "state": "pending", "votes": 21, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Dub", "line": "Bitch, I need to geek up, get a lil’ higher or I’ma get a lil’ pissy", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, I need to geek up, get a lil' higher or I'ma get a lil' pissy"], "meaning": "To get “pissy” means to become annoying or act unusual compared to your normal actions. Yeat is saying he needs to “geek up”, which is get high or he’s going to possibly throw a tantrum, become upset, or get annoyed.\n\nThe term is also used to capitalize off of it’s root-expression, “pissed-off.”\nThis means to become annoyed or have a sense of anger.", "annotation_id": "25564663", "url": "https://genius.com/25564663", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Dub", "line": "(Hey, hey) Bitch playin’ with my wood, she a lumberjack", "vault_lines": ["(Hey, hey) Bitch playin' with my wood, she a lumberjack"], "meaning": "On this line, Yeat is metaphorically expressing the actions of presumably a woman who is touching his penis; hence the usage of the word “playin'”, and is similar to a lumberjack.\n\nA “wood” is slang for a penis so Yeat is saying she’s a lumberjack; someone who deals and works with wood, since their dealing/playing with his wood.", "annotation_id": "25564644", "url": "https://genius.com/25564644", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Dub", "line": "I’m full of red from head to toe (From head to toe)", "vault_lines": ["I'm full of red from head to toe (From head to toe)"], "meaning": "We’re all full of red from head to toe with blood, but here Yeat plays on that and implies that his body composition has come to consist of pints on pints of red cough syrup.", "annotation_id": "25484969", "url": "https://genius.com/25484969", "state": "pending", "votes": 26, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
