{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Down badd", "line": "(Mino)", "vault_lines": ["I was so down bad, I was in the dirt (Dirt)"], "meaning": "sampled from splatoon 2.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/vvyh3I8mwXM?si=wfzhLnHvhpRoCbwl", "annotation_id": "39956900", "url": "https://genius.com/39956900", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Down badd", "line": "I’m not lyin’ talkin’ bout these pills, why would I do that? (Yeah) That’d be like Virgil makin’ a shoelace, callin’ it shoelack (What the fuck?)", "vault_lines": ["I'm not lyin' talkin' bout these pills, why would I do that? (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Yeat is emphasizing authenticity, he’s not lying about popping pills because that lifestyle is already well documented in his music and persona. Flexing about drugs isn’t impressive if it’s fake, so there would be no reason for him to exaggerate it.\n\nHe compares the idea to legendary designer Virgil Abloh, whose Off-White brand is known for labeling items literally (like shoelaces saying “SHOELACES”). If Virgil made a shoelace and called it “shoelack,” it would be pointlessly incorrect just like lying about pills would be pointlessly fake. This is Yeat reinforcing that what he raps about is real, while also referencing high fashion culture.", "annotation_id": "37439910", "url": "https://genius.com/37439910", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Down badd", "line": "I used to wanna be a scientist, I like opium flowers (Oxy’)", "vault_lines": ["I used to wanna be a scientist, I like opium flowers (Oxy')"], "meaning": "Here, Yeat is reflecting on his past. He says he once had aspirations to be a scientist, which is an interesting contrast to his current lifestyle. The phrase “I like opium flowers” is likely a reference to his use of opiates, often symbolized by poppies (from which opium is derived). The mention of “Oxy'” refers to OxyContin, a powerful painkiller, often associated with drug use.", "annotation_id": "34992926", "url": "https://genius.com/34992926", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Down badd", "line": "If you was hatin’ on the gang, I hope you don’t kiss your kids “Goodnight”", "vault_lines": ["If you was hatin' on the gang, I hope you don't kiss your kids \"Goodnight\""], "meaning": "In the line, “If you was hatin' on the gang, I hope you don’t kiss your kids goodnight,” from Yeat’s song “Down Badd,” the artist is expressing a powerful feeling about disrespect or disloyalty. In short, Yeat is communicating that if someone has hated on him or his group, they may not be able to comfortably or safely do something as normal as kiss their kids goodnight. The implication is that the consequences of their hate could be serious, or lead to serious consequences, for not just that person, but for their entire family, family, because of the disrespect, even if unintended. It’s a metaphor, but illustrates how serious he feels about loyalty, and how serious the consequences can become.", "annotation_id": "34992844", "url": "https://genius.com/34992844", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Down badd", "line": "All my pockets blue, but they closed, like they Lilo Stitched (Stitched)", "vault_lines": ["All my pockets blue, but they closed, like they Lilo Stitched (Stitched)"], "meaning": "It’s obvious Yeat has a thing for talking about aliens, but he references the classical film, Lilo and Stitch, because of the main garbling character Stitch, also known as Experiment 626. The creature has difficulty talking in comprehensive English due to him being an extraterrestrial alien, and his fur is— visibly blue.\n\nTherefore, the reference is that there are a quantity of hundred dollar value blue-stripped bills stuffed inside of what seems to be his pant pockets, which are inferred to have zippers on them to remain closed.", "annotation_id": "34059997", "url": "https://genius.com/34059997", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Down badd", "line": "You can take this F from this Fendi, ’fore I fuck it up You can take this L from this Louis, ’fore I luggage up (Luggage up) You can take the Y from the Yeat and she still eat it up", "vault_lines": ["You can take this F from this Fendi, 'fore I fuck it up (Fuck it up)"], "meaning": "Yeat describes two things the listener can take away from his music as a whole; the F from his mentioning of Fendi shoes before he quite literally, F’s them up, along with the L inside of the main crossed letter logo of the designer brand, Louis Vuitton before he places his valuables inside of it.\n\nThe listener’s female partner, however, may not resist having sexual relations containing oral sex with Yeat, even amongst the removal of Y from his name turning him to “Eat”.\n\nThis subtle acrostic spells out “FLY”, which may have been planned to allude to how high he gets during his studio sessions.", "annotation_id": "34059982", "url": "https://genius.com/34059982", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
