{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "I watched them all bend, fold up like a crease", "vault_lines": ["I watched them all bend, fold up like a crease"], "meaning": "Yeat describes others breaking under pressure or submitting when confronted with his success. The imagery highlights weakness in contrast to his resilience and authority.", "annotation_id": "38603250", "url": "https://genius.com/38603250", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "I watched them all bend, fold up like a crease", "vault_lines": ["I watched them all bend, fold up like a crease"], "meaning": "Yeat describes others breaking under pressure or submitting when confronted with his success. The imagery highlights weakness in contrast to his resilience and authority.", "annotation_id": "38603247", "url": "https://genius.com/38603247", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "Yeah, I’m the general, I’m the chief, CEO man", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I'm the general, I'm the chief, CEO man"], "meaning": "Yeat stacks titles to reinforce authority and control, but this line also connects to personas he’s referenced before, such as “General Twat” or “Twat General,” which reflect his abrasive, confrontational attitude. The “CEO” title ties into his later 2093-era identity, often described as the Psycho CEO, where he frames himself as a cold, calculating leader running his life, music, and brand like a ruthless corporation. Together, the line blends irony, self-mythology, and dominance, presenting Yeat as both chaotic and in total command.", "annotation_id": "38603244", "url": "https://genius.com/38603244", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "Yeah, I’m the general, I’m the chief, CEO man", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, I'm the general, I'm the chief, CEO man"], "meaning": "Yeat stacks titles to reinforce authority and control, but this line also connects to personas he’s referenced before, such as “General Twat” or “Twat General,” which reflect his abrasive, confrontational attitude. The “CEO” title ties into his later 2093-era identity, often described as the Psycho CEO, where he frames himself as a cold, calculating leader running his life, music, and brand like a ruthless corporation. Together, the line blends irony, self-mythology, and dominance, presenting Yeat as both chaotic and in total command.", "annotation_id": "38603243", "url": "https://genius.com/38603243", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "If we so dead, then why we so rich and why we so alive? (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["If we so dead, then why we so rich and why we so alive? (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Yeat flips criticism back on his detractors. Being labeled “dead” creatively or spiritually contradicts his financial success and lifestyle, which he treats as proof of dominance.", "annotation_id": "38603208", "url": "https://genius.com/38603208", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "If we so dead, then why we so rich and why we so alive? (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["If we so dead, then why we so rich and why we so alive? (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Yeat flips criticism back on his detractors. Being labeled “dead” creatively or spiritually contradicts his financial success and lifestyle, which he treats as proof of dominance.", "annotation_id": "38603201", "url": "https://genius.com/38603201", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "Juice in my cup", "vault_lines": ["Juice in my cup"], "meaning": "Here, “juice” specifically refers to lean, a drink made from prescription cough syrup mixed with soda. Yeat uses it to signal intoxication while tying drug use into his luxury lifestyle, a recurring theme in his music where excess, wealth, and altered states blend together.", "annotation_id": "38603200", "url": "https://genius.com/38603200", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "In the Tonka truck and I can’t crash", "vault_lines": ["In the Tonka truck and I can't crash"], "meaning": "“Tonka truck” exaggerates the size and power of his vehicle. Saying he can’t crash implies invincibility, confidence, and the idea that wealth insulates him from consequences.", "annotation_id": "38603198", "url": "https://genius.com/38603198", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "I got juice in my cup (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I got juice in my cup (Yeah)"], "meaning": "Here, “juice” specifically refers to lean, a drink made from prescription cough syrup mixed with soda. Yeat uses it to signal intoxication while tying drug use into his luxury lifestyle, a recurring theme in his music where excess, wealth, and altered states blend together.", "annotation_id": "38603195", "url": "https://genius.com/38603195", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "Don’t understand how I got this minky on me, might as well call this shit fleece", "vault_lines": ["Don't understand how I got this minky on me, might as well call this shit fleece"], "meaning": "“Minky” and “fleece” reference soft, luxury fabrics, symbolizing expensive clothing and comfort. Yeat suggests wealth surrounds him so naturally that even he barely questions it anymore.", "annotation_id": "38603189", "url": "https://genius.com/38603189", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "Only bread you got is sandwiches, we gon call you", "vault_lines": ["Only bread you got is sandwiches, we gon call you"], "meaning": "“Bread” is slang for money. Yeat mocks others for having no real wealth, flipping food imagery to suggest their money is fake or insignificant compared to his. Calling it “caprese” keeps the flex playful while still dismissive.", "annotation_id": "38603184", "url": "https://genius.com/38603184", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "We gon’ leave you out to hang like a hammock", "vault_lines": ["We gon' leave you out to hang like a hammock"], "meaning": "When Yeat says “We gon’ leave you out to hang like a hammock”, he’s using metaphorical imagery to describe isolating someone completely. “Left out to hang” implies being abandoned or cut off, while comparing it to a hammock adds a darkly casual tone, like it’s effortless or routine. The line suggests that once someone is no longer useful or loyal, Yeat has no problem leaving them behind without concern.", "annotation_id": "38603169", "url": "https://genius.com/38603169", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "You got your hand out so much, we chop your hand then", "vault_lines": ["You got your hand out so much, we chop your hand then"], "meaning": "When Yeat says “You got your hand out so much, we chop your hand then”, he’s using exaggerated, violent imagery to talk about cutting off freeloaders. “Hand out” refers to people constantly asking for money, favors, or clout without contributing anything. “Chop your hand” isn’t literal, but a metaphor for shutting them out completely, cutting access, or removing them from his circle. The line reinforces Yeat’s theme of self-reliance and intolerance for leeches once success is achieved.", "annotation_id": "38603163", "url": "https://genius.com/38603163", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "DISEASE", "line": "(All white party, get in, so I’ll pull up with that priest)", "vault_lines": ["All white party, get in, so I'll pull up with that priest"], "meaning": "When Yeat says “All white party, get in, so I’ll pull up with that priest”, he’s referencing Michael Rubin’s exclusive all-white Fourth of July party. The event is known for its strict white dress code and elite guest list. “Pull up with that priest” plays on the all-white theme, comparing Yeat’s outfit to a priest’s robes while adding irony by mixing religious imagery with a high-luxury, celebrity LYFESTYLE. The line flexes access to elite spaces while emphasizing how he stands out even among the rich and famous.", "annotation_id": "38603124", "url": "https://genius.com/38603124", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
