{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "BË QUIET", "line": "Fuck all that lil’ extra shit (Woo), you know the God back", "vault_lines": ["Fuck all that lil' extra shit (Woo), you know the God back"], "meaning": "Ba'al – an ancient deity revered in various cultures, embodies the duality of nature—both a nurturer and a destroyer. Often associated with storms and fertility, he symbolizes the power of life and the chaos that can arise from it. His complex legacy weaves themes of devotion, conflict, and the eternal struggle between order and chaos.", "annotation_id": "33319424", "url": "https://genius.com/33319424", "state": "pending", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "BË QUIET", "line": "(Fuck, fuck, give me that back), fuck, fuck, fuck, fuck", "vault_lines": ["Ooh, ooh (Fuck, fuck, give me that back), fuck, fuck, fuck, fuck (King Tonk')"], "meaning": "The repeated use of “fuck” in the intro is a signature ad-lib of LAZER DIM 700. He often opens his tracks with multiple iterations of the word, notably on his viral hit, “Laced Max”.\n\nIn an interview with The FADER on the album, Yeat’s manager, Zack Bia, said this about the ad-lib:\n\n Without the ‘fawk’ it sounds empty. It’s almost like [he’s] an instrument, it really filled out the beat.", "annotation_id": "33319022", "url": "https://genius.com/33319022", "state": "accepted", "votes": 91, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
