{"type": "song", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Bring It In", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Yeat-bring-it-in-lyrics", "genius_title": "Bring It In", "genius_id": 10850871, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "I said fuck love, bitch, you need to tug (Ha-ha)", "vault_lines": ["I said fuck love, bitch, you need to tug (Ha-ha)"], "meaning": "Yeat, in his alter-ego here as Psycho CEO, is informing his female companion that he isn’t interested in romance or any sort of deeper relationship with her. He just wants her to jack him off. This fits with the overall characterization of Psycho CEO as a hedonistic narcissist, who cares first and foremost about his own pleasure and desires.", "annotation_id": "31883397", "url": "https://genius.com/31883397", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "I hit home runs, bitch, you boutta bunt (Ha-ha)", "vault_lines": ["I hit home runs, bitch, you boutta bunt (Ha-ha)"], "meaning": "Yeat is batting in the rap game better than his peers. To ‘bunt’ in baseball is to simply tap the ball into the infield instead of actually swinging to hit it. Often resulting in a foul, bunting is considered by many in the baseball community a bad play. Yeat, on the other hand, is making hits and home runs all the way around the field.", "annotation_id": "31008117", "url": "https://genius.com/31008117", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "I know I’m a asshole (Ha-ha) I know when I dip and go (Ha-ha) I know when I lose control (Ha-ha)", "vault_lines": ["I know I'm a asshole (Ha-ha)"], "meaning": "Yeat knows himself but other people don’t. When he said dip and go he’s hinting at the fact he hadn’t dropped an album since 2023 in February which is almost a year to this release and dropped about 3 singles in the span of that year.", "annotation_id": "30989505", "url": "https://genius.com/30989505", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "Heard you went up, now you goin’ down", "vault_lines": ["Heard you went up, now you goin' down (Yeah)"], "meaning": "These lyrics serve as a double entendre that artfully parallels the fleeting nature of success in both the rap industry and financial markets like cryptocurrency or stocks. In the context of a rap career, “going up” symbolizes an artist’s rise to fame and success, while “going down” suggests an impending decline, advising the artist to “cash out” or secure their gains while at the peak. Simultaneously, these words mirror the volatile dynamics of financial investments, where “going up” refers to an increase in value, and “going down” indicates a market downturn, with “cash out” serving as prudent advice to liquidate at a high point to avoid potential losses. This clever wordplay emphasizes the impermanence of success in both domains, advising strategic foresight and action when one is at the pinnacle.", "annotation_id": "30974174", "url": "https://genius.com/30974174", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "I’ma cut it open, I’m doin’ surgery In the limelight, make ’em go insane Yeah, make ’em go insane", "vault_lines": ["I'ma cut it open, I'm doin' surgery"], "meaning": "Limelight is bright green lighting used by surgeons to illuminate the operating area, allowing for better contrast while working on the human anatomy. The lighting is also proven to cut fatigue and provide visual comfort in the operating room during critical procedures.\n\n\n\nPictured: One of Riverside Walter Reed Hospital's limelight operating rooms\n\n\nThis green light does not make people insane, but could instead point to the fact that popular figures are under constant scrutiny for their actions, eventually leading to their breaking point. While fame may offer temporary benefits, it actually deteriorates the individual beneath as seen in many celebrities.\n\nBeing “in the limelight” can also denote a public figure prominent in the surrounding world’s eye, one example being the media, constantly scrutinizing celebrities for their opinions and beliefs, observed in a University at Albany study.", "annotation_id": "30963086", "url": "https://genius.com/30963086", "state": "accepted", "votes": 23, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "I won’t ever give you time to (Breathe)", "vault_lines": ["I won't ever give you time to (Breathe)"], "meaning": "This could be implied as Yeat applying so much pressure, whether it be in his music or to people who oppose him, that it metaphorically (or literally) will not give his opposition the time to breathe.", "annotation_id": "30929262", "url": "https://genius.com/30929262", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "(Ha-ha-ha-ha) (Ha-ha-ha-ha) (Ha-ha-ha-ha) (Ha-ha-ha-ha)", "vault_lines": ["I told you, I lost all feeling (Ha-ha-ha)"], "meaning": "This is the common outro of a Yeat song as he usually does some throat clears and makes some “Villain Like Sounds” at the end of the song.", "annotation_id": "30928994", "url": "https://genius.com/30928994", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "I made a fuck ton of money, you make nun’ (Ha-ha)", "vault_lines": ["I made a fuck ton of money, you make nun' (Ha-ha)"], "meaning": "Here Yeat degrades his opps by saying that they made no money and he, on the other hand, made a lot of money by doing his shit.", "annotation_id": "30928991", "url": "https://genius.com/30928991", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "Got the system on overload (Yeah) (Ha-ha)", "vault_lines": ["Got the system on overload (Yeah) (Ha-ha)"], "meaning": "Here Yeat says how he fucked the whole system (often used as an alternative for the music industry) by dropping his awaited album “2093”.", "annotation_id": "30928984", "url": "https://genius.com/30928984", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "I recommend you cash out (Yeah)", "vault_lines": ["I recommend you cash out (Yeah)"], "meaning": "These lyrics serve as a double entendre that artfully parallels the fleeting nature of success in both the rap industry and financial markets like cryptocurrency or stocks. In the context of a rap career, “going up” symbolizes an artist’s rise to fame and success, while “going down” suggests an impending decline, advising the artist to “cash out” or secure their gains while at the peak. Simultaneously, these words mirror the volatile dynamics of financial investments, where “going up” refers to an increase in value, and “going down” indicates a market downturn, with “cash out” serving as prudent advice to liquidate at a high point to avoid potential losses. This clever wordplay emphasizes the impermanence of success in both domains, advising strategic foresight and action when one is at the pinnacle.\n\nThe brilliance of the double entendre lies in its ability to simultaneously convey advice in both the personal and financial spheres, emphasizing the transient nature of success in both fields. It serves as a cautionary tale, advising individuals to be aware of the peaks and troughs in their careers and financial endeavors and to make strategic decisions when they are at the top, as downturns are often inevitable.", "annotation_id": "30927362", "url": "https://genius.com/30927362", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "(Ha-ha) Breathe (Ha-ha) Breathe (Ha-ha) Breathe (Ha-ha) Breathe", "vault_lines": ["I won't breathe, 'cause it's fun (Ha)"], "meaning": "Adding on to Song’s Intro; Produced by ​bart how, he flips the beat by taking sample from REGULAR SHOW: SEASON 3 – EPISODE 7’s Basketball God.\n\nMoreover, its pretty unique to see the fusion of Electro Indian Dhol (Barrel drum) in Yeat’s flavor throughout the song.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/E0vZZfDvC70", "annotation_id": "30926457", "url": "https://genius.com/30926457", "state": "pending", "votes": 24, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "Got ‘em turnin’ they heads", "vault_lines": ["Got 'em turnin' they heads"], "meaning": "Saying you “turn heads” is to say that you’re so eccentric that people turn their heads to stare at you when you walk by. Yeat is saying that he’s got the people he walks by turning their heads to look at him because of how he looks and/or dresses.", "annotation_id": "30925248", "url": "https://genius.com/30925248", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "And you make your money taller than the ceiling", "vault_lines": ["And you make your money taller than the ceiling"], "meaning": "Rappers often have lyrics comparing the metaphorical height of their money, if they hypothetically stacked it all up, to other things. In this case, Yeat’s telling whoever he’s talking to that they have the potential to make their money stack up taller than the ceiling of a room.", "annotation_id": "30925224", "url": "https://genius.com/30925224", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "I was bored, bought a jet, made a billion (Breathe) Nah, fuck that, bitch, I need a trillion (Breathe) Nah, fuck that, bitch, I need to feel it (Breathe) Made me so damn mad, made me feel it (Breathe)", "vault_lines": ["I was bored, bought a jet, made a billion (Breathe)"], "meaning": "In these lyrics, Yeat expresses a desire for continuous growth and ambition. He reflects on achieving significant wealth, symbolized by “made a billion.” However, rather than being content with this accomplishment, Yeat dismisses it as insignificant, declaring that he now craves even greater success, symbolized by “Nah fuck that, bitch, I need a trillion.” This line underscores his relentless pursuit of success and highlights his dissatisfaction with settling for anything less than the highest possible achievement. The repetition of “Nah fuck that” emphasizes his determination and refusal to be complacent, as he seeks to push himself further to fulfill his ambitions and desires.", "annotation_id": "30922251", "url": "https://genius.com/30922251", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "Made me so damn mad, made me feel it (Breathe)", "vault_lines": ["Made me so damn mad, made me feel it (Breathe)"], "meaning": "It made him so mad that through the highs and money that come from what he earns he was still able to feel the anger through it all", "annotation_id": "30918120", "url": "https://genius.com/30918120", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "Yeah, when you level up, you makin’ money, billion", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, when you level up, you makin' money, billion"], "meaning": "When you “level up” which could be referring to leveling up your own skills, you are able to make more money by using those “leveled up” skills hence “when you level up, you makin' money, billion”", "annotation_id": "30918110", "url": "https://genius.com/30918110", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "I won’t shake your hand, bitch, you need a glove (Ha-ha)", "vault_lines": ["I won't shake your hand, bitch, you need a glove (Ha-ha)"], "meaning": "He refuses to shake your hand because he doesn’t know where your hand has been and he knows that he is better than you (the bitch)", "annotation_id": "30918096", "url": "https://genius.com/30918096", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "I told you, I lost all feeling (Ha-ha-ha)", "vault_lines": ["I told you, I lost all feeling (Ha-ha-ha)"], "meaning": "In this lyric, Yeat expresses a sense of emotional detachment or numbness. He suggests that he has become desensitized to certain emotions or experiences, indicating a loss of sensitivity or connection. The use of “Ha-ha” after the statement could imply a sense of irony or resignation, as if he finds the situation amusing or ironic despite the underlying seriousness of the sentiment.", "annotation_id": "30898594", "url": "https://genius.com/30898594", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "And as far as I could tell, they wanna sound like me (Breathe), they wanna be like me", "vault_lines": ["And as far as I could tell, they wanna sound like me (Breathe), they wanna be like me"], "meaning": "In this lyric, Yeat reflects on his influence and prominence in the music industry. He suggests that others aspire to emulate his style and success, indicating that they want to replicate his sound and persona. This line conveys a sense of confidence and pride in his unique identity and artistic vision. Additionally, it highlights the desire for recognition and acknowledgment of his impact on the music scene.", "annotation_id": "30898587", "url": "https://genius.com/30898587", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "Wash the blood off my hands, I need Dove (Ha-ha)", "vault_lines": ["Wash the blood off my hands, I need Dove (Ha-ha)"], "meaning": "In this line, Yeat metaphorically refers to the need for purification and cleansing after being involved in morally questionable or harmful actions. “Wash the blood off my hands” symbolizes the desire to rid oneself of guilt or remorse for past deeds, particularly those that may have caused harm to others. The mention of “I need Dove” suggests a specific brand of soap known for its gentle and cleansing properties. This choice of soap emphasizes the need for a thorough and effective cleansing, both physically and metaphorically, to rid oneself of the metaphorical “blood” and its associated guilt or stains.", "annotation_id": "30898577", "url": "https://genius.com/30898577", "state": "pending", "votes": 8, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Breathe", "line": "I was bored, bought a jet, made a billion (Breathe) Nah, fuck that, bitch, I need a trillion (Breathe) Nah, fuck that, bitch, I need to feel it (Breathe)", "vault_lines": ["I was bored, bought a jet, made a billion (Breathe)"], "meaning": "In these lyrics, Yeat expresses a desire for continuous growth and ambition. He reflects on achieving significant wealth, symbolized by “made a billion.” However, rather than being content with this accomplishment, Yeat dismisses it as insignificant, declaring that he now craves even greater success, symbolized by “Nah fuck that, bitch, I need a trillion.” This line underscores his relentless pursuit of success and highlights his dissatisfaction with settling for anything less than the highest possible achievement. The repetition of “Nah fuck that” emphasizes his determination and refusal to be complacent, as he seeks to push himself further to fulfill his ambitions and desires.", "annotation_id": "30898574", "url": "https://genius.com/30898574", "state": "pending", "votes": 31, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
