{"type": "song", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Back homë", "status": "done", "targets": 6, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Yeat-back-home-lyrics", "genius_title": "Back homë", "genius_id": 8431990, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Back Home", "line": "I know that you want it, you need it I poke and I prod ‘til you bleedin’ I know, I wanna go back (Home)", "vault_lines": ["I know that you want it, you need it"], "meaning": "Yeat is describing an intense and almost obsessive desire between himself and another person. “I know that you want it, you need it” suggests that he is confident the other person is drawn toward something he can provide, whether that is his attention, lifestyle, or the excitement surrounding him. “I poke and I prod ’til you bleedin’” uses exaggerated physical imagery to describe continuously pushing someone’s boundaries or provoking them until they reach their limit. The sudden shift to “I wanna go back (Home)” contrasts that aggression with a more vulnerable feeling, suggesting that despite the chaotic lifestyle and intensity around him, Yeat still has a desire to return to a place where he feels comfortable and secure.", "annotation_id": "41352708", "url": "https://genius.com/41352708", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Back Home", "line": "I just wanna go home", "vault_lines": ["I just wanna go home, I just wanna go home"], "meaning": "In “Back Home” the “home” carries that same bittersweet weight of “Strange Home” ; not just a physical place or pre-fame innocence, but something altered by distance, success, change, and time. The yearning to return collides with the reality that it might feel strange or impossible now (especially with Yeat’s restless statewide motion contrasting Joji’s introspective pull).\n\nSince this track added as the deluxe of Piss in the Wind, it feels like a natural extension of that album’s themes of memory, emotional connection, and fragmented lyrics.\n\nDon’t forget that the home is big thing i joji sons and it started w Slow dancing in the Dark by saying I don’t wanna go home", "annotation_id": "40087456", "url": "https://genius.com/40087456", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Back Home", "line": "Through the storm, I don’t wanna wait", "vault_lines": ["Through the storm, I don't wanna wait"], "meaning": "ready to move through the chaos (Storm) but hoping for something to hold onto.\n\nClassic Joji tension , escape mixed with quiet longing for an savior, like the feelings about return in “Glimpse of Us” or exhaustion and fear of loss in “Ew” and “Run.”", "annotation_id": "40087392", "url": "https://genius.com/40087392", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Back Home", "line": "Yeah, I wanna go back home (Ooh) No, I wanna, I wanna, I wanna, I wanna I wanna go back home (Ooh) (I wanna go back home)", "vault_lines": ["I know, I wanna go back (Home)"], "meaning": "The chorus captures one of the most recurring themes across Yeat’s entire discography: the tension between the life fame built and the simplicity of where he came from. In a rare sit-down interview with Apple Music’s Zane Lowe around ADL’s release, Yeat opened up about being at rock bottom, cutting back significantly on drugs, and prioritizing his health for the first time, a period that made him reflect deeply on where he started.\n\nYeat, born Noah Olivier Smith, is from Portland, Oregon, a city that rarely appears in rap narratives, and the longing to return is both literal and emotional. The fame, the excess, the chaos of the ADL era made “home” feel increasingly distant.\n\nThis is also the second Yeat song with this title. The first “Back Home” appeared on his 2023 album AftërLyfe, making the ADL version a deliberate callback, the same feeling revisited from a different place in life.\n\n\n\nThis time with Joji, whose fragile vocals turn the chorus into something closer to a plea than a flex. A critic noted that Joji turns “Back Home” into something close to a plea, singing “Through the storm, I don’t wanna wait / Please don’t you let me run away.”", "annotation_id": "39850681", "url": "https://genius.com/39850681", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Back Home", "line": "Yeah, I wanna go back home (Ooh) No, I wanna, I wanna, I wanna, I wanna I wanna go back home (Ooh) (I wanna go back home) Ooh (I wanna), I wanna go back home Bitch, I wanna go home, bitch, I wanna go home", "vault_lines": ["I know, I wanna go back (Home)"], "meaning": "Could mean that Mr.Smith might want to go back to his old sound, recent tweets proving it", "annotation_id": "39395205", "url": "https://genius.com/39395205", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Back Home", "line": "I’m a ho, but I don’t want you to see it", "vault_lines": ["I'm a ho, but I don't want you to see it"], "meaning": "Yeat was in a four-year relationship with Symone Ryley, which reportedly came to an end around October, with both later confirming their breakup on December 13, 2024. The split has been linked to allegations of Yeat’s infidelity.\n\n Around 2023-2024 Symone would occasionally post on Instagram hinting Yeat of cheating, only to delete the posts shortly after and claim they were “accidental,” reflecting the confusion, hurt, and underlying tension in their relationship.\n\nThe situation escalated further when adult film actress Violet Myers later spoke publicly about Yeat allegedly messaging her on Instagram with rapper Summrs during a livestream with  42Ceo. she claimed that Yeat had been in contact with her during the filming of music video for his song “GO2WORK” eventually yeat ends up blocking Violet which she expressed frustration over. By bringing the situation to light in the livestream, it intensified the fallout and contributed to the breakdown of Yeat and Symone’s long-term relationship.", "annotation_id": "39225668", "url": "https://genius.com/39225668", "state": "pending", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Back Home", "line": "(Hey)", "vault_lines": ["Or I can leave you there, leave you outside (Hey)"], "meaning": "Joji reinterpreted and incorporated his ad-libs from “Test Drive” into the song.\n\nhttps://youtu.be/JEdtxdqLCs4?t=75", "annotation_id": "39159031", "url": "https://genius.com/39159031", "state": "pending", "votes": 4, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Back Home", "line": "Girl, I’m on my own, and if that’s my home I might never come back, they waiting on me Means nothing at all, the words that I leave", "vault_lines": ["Girl, I'm on my own, and if that's my home"], "meaning": "Joji interpolates his song“R.I.P.” from the album “BALLADS1” here\n\nhttps://youtu.be/qjgMM2UA2To?t=3&is=L7pfYp29T6RQxwey", "annotation_id": "39145350", "url": "https://genius.com/39145350", "state": "pending", "votes": 15, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
