{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Already Said It", "line": "E", "vault_lines": ["UNIK, what are you doing?"], "meaning": "Yeat is likely comparing his success or skill to making consistent three-point shots in basketball. “Hitting that bitch every time from the three” refers to scoring three-pointers reliably. “Givin' that Luka face” refers to Luka Dončić, a famous NBA player known for his impressive plays and expressions after scoring. Yeat is suggesting he’s performing at a high level and confidently, similar to Luka Dončić.", "annotation_id": "31810099", "url": "https://genius.com/31810099", "state": "pending", "votes": -2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Already Said It", "line": "I hit that bitch every time from the three, ooh, givin’ that Luka fac", "vault_lines": ["I hit that bitch every time from the three, ooh, givin' that Luka fac"], "meaning": "Yeat is likely comparing his success or skill to making consistent three-point shots in basketball. “Hitting that bitch every time from the three” refers to scoring three-pointers reliably. “Givin' that Luka face” refers to Luka Dončić, a famous NBA player known for his impressive plays and expressions after scoring. Yeat is suggesting he’s performing at a high level and confidently, similar to Luka Dončić.", "annotation_id": "31810093", "url": "https://genius.com/31810093", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Already Said It", "line": "I’m pushin’ this bitch, I spilled drank on the dash", "vault_lines": ["I'm pushin' this bitch, I spilled drank on the dash"], "meaning": "“…pushin' this bitch” means Summrs is behind the wheel of a car and pushing it to its limits. Summrs says “…spilled drank on the dash” to show that he is driving so fast he has spilled is beverage all over the dashboard of the car.", "annotation_id": "27038790", "url": "https://genius.com/27038790", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Already Said It", "line": "I’m clutchin’ back, clutchin’ that illegal bat", "vault_lines": ["I'm clutchin' back, clutchin' that illegal bat"], "meaning": "This line could mean that Summrs is playing baseball with a bat that is banned by Major League Baseball for being deemed “too good” or providing an unfair advantage to players that wield them. This line could also mean that Summrs is wielding a firearm with illegal modifications, however, this is unlikely as “bat” is not slang for a firearm or weapon.", "annotation_id": "27038713", "url": "https://genius.com/27038713", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Already Said It", "line": "Yeah, Men In Black with a mask up in a Scat", "vault_lines": ["Yeah, Men In Black with a mask up in a Scat"], "meaning": "“Men in Black” is a play on words. Men in Black is a popular action movie, however, Summrs uses this name to show that he and his goons are dressed in all black clothing, likely in an effort to remain unidentified whilst committing crimes. “…with a mask up in a Scat” refers to Summrs and his goons wearing masks, also in an effort to remain unidentified. Summrs also references a Scat Pack which is a variation of the Dodge Charger, a vehicle manufacured by Dodge that has been widely referenced in modern hip-hop and rap.", "annotation_id": "27038692", "url": "https://genius.com/27038692", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Already Said It", "line": "Free Doody, free Boozy, yeah, they up in the chain", "vault_lines": ["Free Doody, free Boozy, yeah, they up in the chain"], "meaning": "Doody and Boozy are likely associates of Summrs'. “…up in the chain” means to be in jail or prison, hence being “chained”. Summrs calls for Doody and Boozy to be freed as they have been imprisoned.", "annotation_id": "27038677", "url": "https://genius.com/27038677", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "Yeat", "song": "Already Said It", "line": "I’m with some felons, ain’t got nun’ to lose", "vault_lines": ["I'm with some felons, ain't got nun' to lose"], "meaning": "Summrs uses this line to show he is associated with dangerous people. “…ain’t got nun' to lose” further elucidates how dangerous Summrs' associates are, implying that they are not afraid to die as they have little to nothing to lose.", "annotation_id": "27038655", "url": "https://genius.com/27038655", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
